chemAddQuestions([
["m8-a-001",8,15,1,"Concept","mcq","Which pair of elements is MOST likely to form an ionic compound?",["K and Br","C and O","N and Cl","Cu and Zn","S and F"],0,"Ionic bonding happens between a metal and a nonmetal: the metal (low ionization energy) transfers electrons to the nonmetal (high electron affinity), and the ions attract. K is a group 1A metal and Br a group 7A nonmetal, so K and Br form ionic KBr. C/O, N/Cl and S/F are pairs of nonmetals, which share electrons (covalent). Cu and Zn are two metals, which pool electrons (metallic bonding).",["Ask whether each element is a metal or a nonmetal.","Ionic = metal + nonmetal (big electronegativity difference, electron transfer).","Only one choice pairs a group 1A metal with a halogen."]],
["m8-a-002",8,15,1,"Concept","mcq","What type of bonding holds the atoms together in a piece of solid copper wire?",["Ionic bonding","Nonpolar covalent bonding","Polar covalent bonding","Hydrogen bonding","Metallic bonding"],4,"Copper is a metal bonded only to other copper atoms, so it is held by metallic bonding: Cu cations in a 'sea' of delocalized valence electrons. Ionic bonding needs a metal plus a nonmetal; covalent bonding is between nonmetals; hydrogen bonding is an intermolecular force involving H on N, O or F, not a bond within a metal.",["Only one element is present - and it is a metal.","Silberberg's three models: ionic (metal + nonmetal), covalent (nonmetals), metallic (metals).","Metal atoms with metal atoms → electron sea."]],
["m8-a-003",8,15,1,"Lecture","mcq","In Silberberg's three bonding models, which description matches IONIC bonding?",["Electrons are shared in pairs between two nonmetal atoms","Electrons are transferred from a metal to a nonmetal, and the resulting ions attract each other","Valence electrons are pooled in a 'sea' shared by all the metal atoms","Electrons are transferred from a nonmetal to a metal, giving metal anions","Two metal atoms share a localized pair of electrons between them"],1,"Ionic model: a metal atom (low IE) loses electrons to a nonmetal atom (high, favourable EA); the cations and anions are held by electrostatic attraction in a lattice. Sharing pairs between nonmetals is covalent bonding; the electron sea is metallic bonding. Metals form cations, not anions, so the 'metal anion' choice reverses the transfer, and metals do not share localized pairs with each other.",["Which kind of atom loses electrons easily?","Ionic = transfer; covalent = sharing; metallic = pooling.","Metal → cation, nonmetal → anion."]],
["m8-a-004",8,15,2,"Concept","mcq","Which substance contains BOTH ionic and covalent bonds?",["NaCl","CO₂","Na₂SO₄","MgBr₂","CH₃OH"],2,"Na₂SO₄ is made of Na⁺ ions and SO₄²⁻ ions (ionic bonding between them), and inside each sulfate ion the S and O atoms share electrons (covalent S−O bonds). NaCl and MgBr₂ are purely ionic (monatomic ions only). CO₂ and CH₃OH are molecules with only covalent bonds.",["Look for a compound that contains a polyatomic ion.","The bonds between the ions are ionic; the bonds inside a polyatomic ion are covalent.","Sodium with sulfate."]],
["m8-a-005",8,15,1,"Concept","num","How many dots appear in the Lewis electron-dot symbol of arsenic (As)? (Enter a whole number.)",["5"],5,"A Lewis dot symbol shows only the valence electrons. Arsenic is in group 5A (15): [Ar] 4s² 3d¹⁰ 4p³, so it has 5 valence electrons (the filled 3d¹⁰ is not valence for a main-group element) → 5 dots.",["Lewis symbols show valence electrons only.","For a main-group element, valence electrons = the A-group number.","As sits under N and P."],{"abs":0}],
["m8-a-006",8,15,2,"Concept","mcq","In its Lewis electron-dot symbol, which atom shows exactly two pairs of dots and two UNPAIRED dots?",["N","Cl","B","O","Ar"],3,"O has 6 valence electrons: dots go one per side first, then pair up, giving 2 pairs + 2 single dots (which is why O usually forms 2 bonds). N (5 e⁻) has 1 pair + 3 singles; Cl (7) has 3 pairs + 1 single; B (3) has 3 singles; Ar (8) has 4 pairs and no unpaired dots.",["Count valence electrons for each atom.","Place dots one per side (four sides) before pairing any.","6 valence electrons → 2 pairs + 2 singles."]],
["m8-a-007",8,15,1,"Concept","mcq","The number of unpaired dots in a main-group atom's Lewis symbol usually equals the number of covalent bonds it forms. How many bonds does nitrogen usually form?",["3","5","1","2","4"],0,"N has 5 valence electrons: 1 pair and 3 unpaired dots, so it usually forms 3 bonds (as in NH₃). 5 counts all valence electrons, not the unpaired ones; 1, 2 and 4 are the usual bond counts of H/halogens, O and C.",["Draw nitrogen's Lewis symbol.","5 valence electrons: one pair plus some single dots.","Think of NH₃."]],
["m8-a-008",8,15,2,"Concept","mcq","Which ion does calcium form in ionic compounds, and why?",["Ca⁺, because only one electron is easy to remove","Ca²⁻, because gaining two electrons fills the 4p sublevel","Ca³⁺, because losing three electrons empties the n = 4 shell and one core electron","Ca²⁺, because losing its two 4s electrons gives the argon configuration","Ca²⁺, because losing two 3d electrons gives a stable half-filled sublevel"],3,"Ca is [Ar] 4s². Losing the two 4s valence electrons gives Ca²⁺, isoelectronic with Ar; the lattice energy gained with a 2+ ion more than repays IE₂. A third electron would come from the argon core (huge IE₃), so Ca³⁺ does not form. Metals lose electrons rather than gain them (no Ca²⁻), and Ca has no 3d electrons at all.",["Write calcium's electron configuration.","Group 2A metals lose their ns² electrons.","[Ar] 4s² → remove 2 electrons."]],
["m8-a-009",8,15,2,"Concept","mcq","What is the formula of the ionic compound formed from aluminum and oxygen?",["AlO","Al₃O₂","AlO₃","Al₂O","Al₂O₃"],4,"Al (group 3A) forms Al³⁺; O (group 6A) forms O²⁻. The charges must cancel: 2(+3) + 3(−2) = 0, so Al₂O₃. Al₃O₂ swaps the subscripts the wrong way (3(+3) + 2(−2) = +5); AlO and AlO₃ and Al₂O are not neutral.",["Find each ion's charge from its group.","Al³⁺ and O²⁻; balance total positive and negative charge.","Lowest common multiple of 3 and 2 is 6."]],
["m8-a-010",8,15,2,"Concept","mcq","Magnesium burns in nitrogen to form an ionic nitride. What is its formula?",["MgN","Mg₂N₃","Mg₃N₂","MgN₂","Mg₂N"],2,"Mg (group 2A) forms Mg²⁺; N (group 5A) gains 3 electrons to form N³⁻. Neutral: 3(+2) + 2(−3) = 0 → Mg₃N₂. Mg₂N₃ reverses the subscripts (2(+2) + 3(−3) = −5); the others are not neutral.",["What charge does N take as a monatomic ion?","Mg²⁺ and N³⁻; criss-cross the charges.","3 × (+2) balances 2 × (−3)."]],
["m8-a-011",8,15,2,"Calculation","num","When one formula unit of Al₂O₃ forms from its atoms, how many electrons in total are transferred from aluminum to oxygen? (Enter a whole number.)",["6"],6,"Each Al atom loses 3 electrons to become Al³⁺; two Al atoms lose 2 × 3 = 6. Check from the other side: three O atoms each gain 2 to become O²⁻, 3 × 2 = 6. Electrons lost = electrons gained = 6.",["Each Al becomes Al³⁺.","Electrons lost by the metal must equal electrons gained by the nonmetal.","2 Al × 3 electrons each."],{"abs":0}],
["m8-a-012",8,15,2,"Concept","mcq","In forming CaCl₂ from its atoms, what happens to the valence electrons?",["One Ca atom transfers two electrons to a single Cl atom, giving Cl²⁻","One Ca atom transfers one electron to each of two Cl atoms","Each Cl atom transfers one electron to the Ca atom","The Ca atom shares one pair of electrons with each Cl atom","Two Ca atoms each transfer one electron to one Cl₂ molecule"],1,"Ca ([Ar] 4s²) loses two electrons; each Cl (7 valence electrons) needs exactly one to reach an octet. So Ca gives one electron to each of two Cl atoms → Ca²⁺ and 2 Cl⁻. Cl²⁻ would put an electron into the next shell; Cl does not donate to a metal; sharing is covalent, not ionic; and the formula CaCl₂ has one Ca, not two.",["How many electrons does each Cl need to reach an octet?","Ca has 2 valence electrons and there are 2 Cl atoms.","One electron for each Cl atom."]],
["m8-a-013",8,15,2,"Concept","mcq","Which statement about the ions in solid NaF is correct?",["Na⁺ is isoelectronic with neon and F⁻ with argon","Na⁺ is isoelectronic with helium and F⁻ with neon","Na⁺ and F⁻ are both isoelectronic with neon","Both ions are isoelectronic with argon","Na⁺ is isoelectronic with neon, but F⁻ still has an unfilled 2p sublevel"],2,"Na (11 e⁻) loses one electron → Na⁺ has 10 electrons, 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ = [Ne]. F (9 e⁻) gains one → F⁻ has 10 electrons, also [Ne]. Neither ion has 18 electrons (argon) or 2 (helium), and F⁻ has a full 2p⁶ sublevel.",["Count the electrons in each ion.","Na⁺: 11 − 1. F⁻: 9 + 1.","Both have 10 electrons."]],
["m8-a-014",8,15,1,"Lecture","mcq","The lattice energy (ΔH°lattice) of an ionic compound is the enthalpy change for:",["forming one mole of the ionic solid from its elements in their standard states","separating one mole of the ionic solid into its gaseous ions","separating one mole of the ionic solid into gaseous atoms","dissolving one mole of the ionic solid in water","melting one mole of the ionic solid"],1,"Silberberg defines ΔH°lattice as the enthalpy change when 1 mol of ionic solid separates into gaseous IONS, e.g. NaCl(s) → Na⁺(g) + Cl⁻(g); it is always positive. Forming the solid from elements is ΔH°f; gaseous atoms (not ions) would skip the ionization and electron-affinity steps; dissolving and melting are different processes.",["The definition ends with gaseous ions.","Write the equation for NaCl.","NaCl(s) → Na⁺(g) + Cl⁻(g)."]],
["m8-a-015",8,15,2,"Trap","mcq","With lattice energy defined as solid → gaseous ions, what is the sign of ΔH°lattice, and what is the sign of ΔH for the Born-Haber step in which the gaseous ions come together to form the solid?",["ΔH°lattice is negative; the ions-to-solid step is positive","Both are positive","Both are negative","ΔH°lattice can be either sign; the ions-to-solid step is always positive","ΔH°lattice is positive; the ions-to-solid step is negative (−ΔH°lattice)"],4,"Pulling oppositely charged ions apart always requires energy, so ΔH°lattice (solid → ions) is positive. The Born-Haber cycle uses the reverse step, ions → solid, which releases the same amount: its ΔH is −ΔH°lattice. That large negative step is what makes ionic solids stable.",["Does it take energy to pull a + and a − apart?","Reversing a process reverses the sign of ΔH.","Separate = endothermic; come together = exothermic."]],
["m8-a-016",8,15,1,"Concept","mcq","According to Coulomb's law, the electrostatic energy of attraction between two ions is:",["directly proportional to the product of their charges and inversely proportional to the distance between them","directly proportional to the distance between them and inversely proportional to the product of their charges","independent of charge and inversely proportional to the distance between them","proportional to the sum of their charges and independent of the distance","proportional to the product of the ions' masses"],0,"Coulomb's law: energy ∝ (charge A × charge B)/distance. Larger charges → stronger attraction; larger distance (bigger ions) → weaker attraction. That is why lattice energy rises with ionic charge and falls with ionic size. Masses play no role.",["Think about what makes two charges attract more strongly.","E ∝ q₁q₂/d.","Product of charges on top, distance on the bottom."]],
["m8-a-017",8,15,2,"Calculation","num","Lattice energy ∝ (q_cation × q_anion)/(r_cation + r_anion). Two ionic solids have the same interionic distance, but the ions in the second carry charges of 2+ and 2− instead of 1+ and 1−. By what factor is the second solid's lattice energy larger? (Enter a number.)",["4"],4,"Only the charge product changes: (2 × 2)/(1 × 1) = 4. Doubling both charges quadruples the attraction (not doubles it), which is why MgO's lattice energy is about four times NaF's.",["Only the numerator changes.","Compare |q₁ × q₂| for the two solids.","(2 × 2) ÷ (1 × 1)."],{"abs":0.01}],
["m8-a-018",8,15,3,"Calculation","num","Using lattice energy ∝ |q₁ × q₂|/d, by what factor would the lattice energy of a solid made of 3+ and 2− ions exceed that of a solid made of 1+ and 1− ions, if the interionic distance d were the same? (Enter a number.)",["6"],6,"Charge product: |(+3)(−2)| = 6 versus |(+1)(−1)| = 1. With the same d, the ratio is 6/1 = 6. Adding the charges (3 + 2 = 5) or squaring them (9 × 4 = 36) are the common mistakes.",["Use the product of the charges, not the sum.","|q₁q₂| = 3 × 2 for the first solid.","The ratio is 6 ÷ 1."],{"abs":0.01}],
["m8-a-019",8,15,2,"Concept","mcq","Which compound has the larger lattice energy, NaCl or KCl, and why?",["KCl, because K⁺ is larger and has more electrons","NaCl, because Na⁺ has a higher charge than K⁺","They are equal, because both cations are 1+ and the anion is the same","NaCl, because Na⁺ is smaller than K⁺, so the ions are closer together","KCl, because K transfers its electron more easily than Na"],3,"Both have 1+ and 1− ions, so the charge product is the same; size decides. Na⁺ is smaller than K⁺ (one fewer shell), so the Na⁺−Cl⁻ distance is shorter and the attraction larger: NaCl ≈ 787 kJ/mol vs KCl ≈ 717 kJ/mol. Na⁺ and K⁺ have the same charge; how easily K ionizes is part of the Born-Haber cycle, not of the lattice energy itself.",["Compare the charges first, then the sizes.","Same charges → smaller ions give a larger lattice energy.","Na⁺ is above K⁺ in group 1A."]],
["m8-a-020",8,15,3,"Exam","mcq","Arrange in order of INCREASING lattice energy: LiF, KBr, NaCl.",["KBr < NaCl < LiF","LiF < NaCl < KBr","NaCl < KBr < LiF","KBr < LiF < NaCl","LiF < KBr < NaCl"],0,"All three are 1+/1− compounds, so ion size decides. KBr has the largest ions (K⁺ and Br⁻) → smallest lattice energy (≈ 680 kJ/mol); NaCl is in between (≈ 787); LiF has the smallest ions → largest (≈ 1050). 'LiF < NaCl < KBr' is the reverse order (the decreasing list).",["All the charges are the same.","Smaller ions → closer together → larger lattice energy.","Smallest ions: Li⁺ and F⁻."]],
["m8-a-021",8,15,3,"Exam","mcq","Arrange in order of DECREASING lattice energy: NaBr, NaF, NaI, NaCl.",["NaI > NaBr > NaCl > NaF","NaF > NaBr > NaCl > NaI","NaCl > NaF > NaBr > NaI","NaF > NaCl > NaI > NaBr","NaF > NaCl > NaBr > NaI"],4,"Same cation and same charges, so the anion size decides: F⁻ < Cl⁻ < Br⁻ < I⁻ in radius, so lattice energy NaF (≈ 923) > NaCl (≈ 787) > NaBr (≈ 747) > NaI (≈ 704 kJ/mol). The reversed list is the increasing order.",["Only the anion changes.","Halide ions get bigger down group 7A.","Smallest anion → largest lattice energy."]],
["m8-a-022",8,15,3,"Exam","mcq","Which list is in order of DECREASING lattice energy?",["BaO > SrO > CaO > MgO","CaO > MgO > SrO > BaO","MgO > CaO > SrO > BaO","MgO > SrO > CaO > BaO","MgO = CaO = SrO = BaO, because every ion is 2+ or 2−"],2,"The charges are all 2+ and 2−, so cation size decides. Down group 2A the cation grows (Mg²⁺ < Ca²⁺ < Sr²⁺ < Ba²⁺), so the distance grows and lattice energy falls: MgO (≈ 3900) > CaO (≈ 3400) > SrO (≈ 3200) > BaO (≈ 3000 kJ/mol). Equal charges do not mean equal lattice energies.",["The charges are identical; what changes?","Cation radius increases down a group.","The smallest cation gives the largest lattice energy."]],
["m8-a-023",8,15,4,"Trap","mcq","The interionic distances in NaF and MgO are similar (about 235 pm and 212 pm). How does the lattice energy of MgO compare with that of NaF?",["About the same, because the ions are nearly the same size","MgO's is roughly four times NaF's, mainly because the product of the ion charges is 4 instead of 1","MgO's is roughly twice NaF's, because each ion's charge is doubled","NaF's is larger, because F is more electronegative than O","MgO's is roughly sixteen times NaF's, because each charge enters squared"],1,"Coulomb: energy ∝ q₁q₂/d. For MgO, (2)(2) = 4; for NaF, (1)(1) = 1. The distances are similar (MgO's is even a little shorter), so MgO's lattice energy is about 4× larger: ≈ 3900 vs ≈ 923 kJ/mol. Doubling each charge multiplies the product by 4, not 2; the charges are not squared individually (that would give 16); electronegativity does not set lattice energy.",["Charge or size - which one differs a lot here?","Use q₁ × q₂ for each compound.","(2 × 2)/(1 × 1) with similar distances."]],
["m8-a-024",8,15,4,"Exam","mcq","Rank in order of INCREASING lattice energy: CaS, KI, MgO, LiCl.",["MgO < CaS < LiCl < KI","LiCl < KI < MgO < CaS","KI < LiCl < MgO < CaS","KI < LiCl < CaS < MgO","LiCl < KI < CaS < MgO"],3,"First sort by charge: KI and LiCl are 1+/1− (charge product 1); CaS and MgO are 2+/2− (product 4), so both 2+/2− solids are far higher. Then sort by size within each pair: KI has larger ions than LiCl, so KI (≈ 650) < LiCl (≈ 850); CaS has larger ions than MgO, so CaS (≈ 3100) < MgO (≈ 3900 kJ/mol). Charge beats size.",["Group the compounds by ion charges first.","Within a charge group, smaller ions → larger lattice energy.","The two 1+/1− salts come first; the oxide with the smallest ions comes last."]],
["m8-a-025",8,15,2,"Concept","mcq","Which compound would you expect to have the HIGHEST melting point?",["NaCl","KBr","MgO","CsI","NaF"],2,"Melting an ionic solid means overcoming the attractions that the lattice energy measures. MgO has 2+ and 2− ions (charge product 4) and small ions, so its lattice energy is by far the largest; it melts near 2850 °C, compared with 993 °C for NaF and 801 °C for NaCl. KBr and CsI have larger, singly charged ions and melt lower still.",["Melting point tracks the strength of the ionic attractions.","Which compound has doubly charged ions?","Highest charge product and small ions."]],
["m8-a-026",8,15,1,"Lecture","mcq","The Born-Haber cycle, which finds a lattice energy from other measured enthalpies, is an application of:",["Coulomb's law alone","Avogadro's law","the law of multiple proportions","Hess's law","the octet rule"],3,"The Born-Haber cycle breaks the formation of an ionic solid from its elements into steps (atomize, ionize, add electrons, form the lattice). Because enthalpy is a state function, the step enthalpies add up to ΔH°f - that is Hess's law. Coulomb's law explains lattice-energy trends but does not by itself give the cycle.",["The cycle adds up the ΔH of several steps.","ΔH of an overall process = sum of the ΔH of its steps.","The Thermochemistry 3 law."]],
["m8-a-027",8,15,1,"Concept","mcq","Which equation represents the ionization step in the Born-Haber cycle for NaCl?",["Na(g) → Na⁺(g) + e⁻","Na(s) → Na⁺(g) + e⁻","Na(s) → Na(g)","Cl(g) + e⁻ → Cl⁻(g)","Na⁺(g) + Cl⁻(g) → NaCl(s)"],0,"Ionization energy is defined for a gaseous atom: Na(g) → Na⁺(g) + e⁻ (IE₁ = +496 kJ/mol). Na(s) → Na(g) is the atomization step that must come first; Na(s) → Na⁺(g) combines two steps; Cl(g) + e⁻ → Cl⁻(g) is the electron-affinity step; the last equation is the lattice-forming step.",["Ionization energy is always for a gaseous atom.","The sodium must already be in the gas phase.","Gaseous atom → gaseous cation + electron."]],
["m8-a-028",8,15,2,"Concept","mcq","Which TWO steps of the Born-Haber cycle for NaCl are exothermic?",["Na(s) → Na(g) and Na(g) → Na⁺(g) + e⁻","½Cl₂(g) → Cl(g) and Cl(g) + e⁻ → Cl⁻(g)","Na(g) → Na⁺(g) + e⁻ and Na⁺(g) + Cl⁻(g) → NaCl(s)","Na(s) → Na(g) and ½Cl₂(g) → Cl(g)","Cl(g) + e⁻ → Cl⁻(g) and Na⁺(g) + Cl⁻(g) → NaCl(s)"],4,"Atomizing Na (+107.8), ionizing Na (+496) and breaking Cl₂ (+121.0 per Cl) all absorb energy. Adding an electron to Cl releases energy (EA = −349 kJ/mol), and bringing the gaseous ions together into the lattice releases a lot (−787 kJ/mol). Those two are the exothermic steps.",["Breaking things apart and removing electrons cost energy.","Chlorine's electron affinity is negative.","Electron addition to Cl, and the ions coming together."]],
["m8-a-029",8,15,2,"Concept","mcq","In a Born-Haber cycle, ΔH for the step ½Cl₂(g) → Cl(g) equals:",["the full Cl−Cl bond energy","one-half of the Cl−Cl bond energy","the electron affinity of Cl","the first ionization energy of Cl","the negative of the Cl−Cl bond energy"],1,"Breaking one mole of Cl−Cl bonds (243 kJ) gives 2 mol of Cl atoms. Only 1 mol of Cl is needed per mole of NaCl, so the step is ½ × 243 ≈ 121.5 kJ (tabulated ΔH°f of Cl(g) = 121.0 kJ/mol). Using the full bond energy is a common error; the step is endothermic, so it is not negative.",["How many Cl atoms come from one Cl₂ molecule?","Bond energy is per mole of Cl−Cl bonds broken.","Half a mole of Cl₂ gives one mole of Cl."]],
["m8-a-030",8,15,3,"Calculation","num","Use a Born-Haber cycle to find the lattice energy of NaCl. Data (kJ/mol): ΔH°f of NaCl(s) = −411.1; Na(s) → Na(g), +107.8; first ionization energy of Na, +496; ½Cl₂(g) → Cl(g), +121.0; electron affinity of Cl, −349. (Enter a number in kJ/mol.)",["787 kJ/mol"],786.9,"Hess's law: ΔH°f = ΔH(atomize Na) + IE₁ + ΔH(½Cl₂ → Cl) + EA + (−ΔH°lattice). So −411.1 = 107.8 + 496 + 121.0 − 349 − ΔH°lattice = 375.8 − ΔH°lattice → ΔH°lattice = 375.8 + 411.1 = 786.9 ≈ 787 kJ/mol. It must come out positive (separating ions costs energy).",["Write ΔH°f as the sum of all five steps.","The lattice-forming step enters as −ΔH°lattice.","ΔH°lattice = 107.8 + 496 + 121.0 − 349 + 411.1."]],
["m8-a-031",8,15,3,"Calculation","mcq","Calculate the lattice energy of LiF from these data (kJ/mol): ΔH°f of LiF(s) = −617; Li(s) → Li(g), +161; IE₁ of Li, +520; F−F bond energy, +159; electron affinity of F, −328.",["1129 kJ/mol","1706 kJ/mol","−185 kJ/mol","−1050 kJ/mol","1050 kJ/mol"],4,"Steps: Li(s) → Li(g) +161; Li(g) → Li⁺(g) +520; ½F₂ → F(g) +½(159) = +79.5; F(g) + e⁻ → F⁻(g) −328; ions → LiF(s) = −ΔH°lattice. Sum = ΔH°f: 161 + 520 + 79.5 − 328 − ΔH°lattice = −617 → ΔH°lattice = 432.5 + 617 = 1049.5 ≈ 1050 kJ/mol. 1129 uses the whole F−F energy instead of half; 1706 adds the electron affinity as +328; −185 uses +617 for ΔH°f; −1050 has the lattice-energy sign reversed.",["Only half a mole of F₂ is needed.","ΔH°f = sum of steps, with the lattice step entering as −ΔH°lattice.","ΔH°lattice = 161 + 520 + 79.5 − 328 + 617."]],
["m8-a-032",8,15,4,"Exam","mcq","Use a Born-Haber cycle to calculate ΔH°f of KCl(s). Data (kJ/mol): K(s) → K(g), +89; IE₁ of K, +419; Cl−Cl bond energy, +242; electron affinity of Cl, −349; lattice energy of KCl, +717.",["−437 kJ/mol","+997 kJ/mol","+261 kJ/mol","−316 kJ/mol","−856 kJ/mol"],0,"ΔH°f = ΔH(K(s) → K(g)) + IE₁ + ½BE(Cl₂) + EA − ΔH°lattice = 89 + 419 + 121 − 349 − 717 = −437 kJ/mol. +997 adds the lattice energy instead of subtracting it (the ions-to-solid step releases energy); +261 uses +349 for the electron affinity; −316 uses the whole Cl−Cl energy (242) instead of half; −856 leaves out the ionization energy.",["Formation of the solid from the elements is the sum of all steps.","The final step (ions → solid) is −ΔH°lattice; use ½ of the Cl−Cl energy.","89 + 419 + 121 − 349 − 717."]],
["m8-a-033",8,15,4,"Exam","mcq","Use a Born-Haber cycle to find the electron affinity of chlorine. Data (kJ/mol): ΔH°f of KCl(s) = −437; K(s) → K(g), +89; IE₁ of K, +419; Cl−Cl bond energy, +243; lattice energy of KCl, +717.",["−1783.5 kJ/mol","−471.0 kJ/mol","−349.5 kJ/mol","+349.5 kJ/mol","+69.5 kJ/mol"],2,"ΔH°f = ΔH(K(s) → K(g)) + IE₁ + ½BE(Cl₂) + EA − ΔH°lattice, so −437 = 89 + 419 + 121.5 + EA − 717. EA = −437 − 89 − 419 − 121.5 + 717 = −349.5 kJ/mol (exothermic, as expected for Cl). −1783.5 treats the ions-to-solid step as +717; −471.0 uses the whole Cl−Cl energy; +69.5 leaves out IE₁; +349.5 has the sign flipped at the end.",["Write ΔH°f as the sum of the five steps, with EA as the unknown.","Use ½ × 243 for Cl atoms and −717 for the ions-to-solid step.","EA = −437 − 89 − 419 − 121.5 + 717."]],
["m8-a-034",8,15,4,"Exam","mcq","Find the lattice energy of MgO(s). Data (kJ/mol): ΔH°f of MgO(s) = −601.6; Mg(s) → Mg(g), +148; IE₁ of Mg, +738; IE₂ of Mg, +1451; O=O bond energy, +498; first electron affinity of O, −141; second electron affinity of O (O⁻ → O²⁻), +878.",["2474 kJ/mol","2169 kJ/mol","4174 kJ/mol","3925 kJ/mol","2721 kJ/mol"],3,"Steps: 148 (atomize Mg) + 738 + 1451 (two ionizations) + 249 (½ × 498 to make one O atom) − 141 + 878 (two electron additions; adding an electron to O⁻ is endothermic) − ΔH°lattice = −601.6. So ΔH°lattice = 3323 + 601.6 = 3924.6 ≈ 3925 kJ/mol. 2474 forgets IE₂; 2169 treats the second EA as −878; 4174 uses the full O=O energy; 2721 uses +601.6 for ΔH°f.",["Mg²⁺ needs two ionization steps and O²⁻ needs two electron-addition steps.","Only ½ mol O₂ is needed; the second electron affinity is positive.","ΔH°lattice = 148 + 738 + 1451 + 249 − 141 + 878 + 601.6."]],
["m8-a-035",8,15,4,"Calculation","mcq","Find the lattice energy of CaCl₂(s). Data (kJ/mol): ΔH°f of CaCl₂(s) = −795.8; Ca(s) → Ca(g), +178; IE₁ of Ca, +590; IE₂ of Ca, +1145; Cl−Cl bond energy, +243; electron affinity of Cl, −349.",["2603 kJ/mol","2254 kJ/mol","2132 kJ/mol","1109 kJ/mol","662 kJ/mol"],1,"One mole of CaCl₂ needs a whole mole of Cl₂ (two Cl atoms) and two electron additions. ΔH°f = 178 + 590 + 1145 + 243 + 2(−349) − ΔH°lattice → −795.8 = 1458 − ΔH°lattice → ΔH°lattice = 2253.8 ≈ 2254 kJ/mol. 2603 uses only one electron affinity; 2132 uses ½(243) as if only one Cl were needed; 1109 forgets IE₂; 662 uses +795.8 for ΔH°f.",["Count how many Cl atoms are in one formula unit.","Two Cl atoms: the full Cl−Cl bond energy and 2 × EA.","ΔH°lattice = 178 + 590 + 1145 + 243 − 698 + 795.8."]],
["m8-a-036",8,15,2,"Concept","mcq","The first ionization energy of Na (+496 kJ/mol) is larger in size than the energy released when Cl gains an electron (−349 kJ/mol). Why is forming NaCl(s) from Na(s) and Cl₂(g) still strongly exothermic?",["Electron transfer from a Na atom to a Cl atom is itself exothermic","Breaking the Cl−Cl bond releases energy","The large energy released when the gaseous ions form the crystal lattice outweighs the net cost of making the ions","Sublimation of sodium releases energy","Na⁺ and Cl⁻ have noble-gas configurations, and reaching one always releases energy"],2,"Making the gaseous ions costs net energy: 496 − 349 = +147 kJ/mol (plus atomizing both elements). The payoff is the lattice step: gaseous ions → solid releases about 787 kJ/mol, which is why ΔH°f(NaCl) = −411 kJ/mol. Electron transfer between isolated atoms is endothermic; breaking bonds and subliming absorb energy; reaching a noble-gas configuration does not by itself release energy (forming Na⁺ costs energy).",["Add up IE and EA: is the transfer alone favourable?","Which Born-Haber step is very large and negative?","Think about the ions coming together."]],
["m8-a-037",8,15,3,"Concept","mcq","Why does sodium form Na⁺ but never Na²⁺ in ionic compounds?",["Removing a second electron would take a core 2p electron, which costs far more energy than the extra lattice energy could repay","Na has only one electron in total outside its nucleus","A 2+ ion would be too small to fit in a crystal lattice","Na²⁺ would be isoelectronic with neon, which is unstable","Chloride ions can accept only one electron each, so Na can give only one"],0,"Na is [Ne] 3s¹. The first electron (3s) is easy to remove (496 kJ/mol), but the second would come from the filled n = 2 core (IE₂ ≈ 4560 kJ/mol). No increase in lattice energy can pay that back, so compounds of Na²⁺ do not form. Na has 11 electrons; it is Na⁺ (not Na²⁺) that is isoelectronic with Ne; and a metal could give electrons to more anions if it were favourable (as Mg does).",["Write Na's configuration and look at what the second electron would be.","Compare IE₁ with IE₂ - core electrons are very hard to remove.","The second electron is in the [Ne] core."]],
["m8-a-038",8,15,4,"Trap","mcq","Forming Mg²⁺ costs IE₁ + IE₂ = 2189 kJ/mol, far more than forming Mg⁺ (738 kJ/mol), and making O²⁻ from O⁻ is also endothermic. Why is MgO still made of Mg²⁺ and O²⁻ ions rather than Mg⁺ and O⁻?",["Adding the second electron to O⁻ releases a lot of energy","The second ionization energy of magnesium is negative","Mg⁺O⁻ would have the larger lattice energy, but it forms too slowly","Mg⁺ and O⁻ ions cannot exist, even in the gas phase","With 2+ and 2− ions the lattice energy is roughly four times larger, which more than repays the extra ionization and electron-addition costs"],4,"Lattice energy ∝ q₁q₂/d: going from (1)(1) to (2)(2) multiplies it by about 4 (≈ 3900 kJ/mol for MgO). That gain is far bigger than the extra cost of IE₂ (1451) plus the endothermic second electron addition (+878), so the 2+/2− solid has the lower total enthalpy. The second electron affinity of O is positive, not exothermic; IE₂ is positive; Mg⁺ and O⁻ do exist as gaseous ions.",["Which term grows a lot when both charges double?","Coulomb: (2 × 2) vs (1 × 1).","The bigger lattice energy pays for IE₂ and the second electron addition."]],
["m8-a-039",8,15,1,"Concept","mcq","Which property is typical of ionic compounds such as NaCl?",["They conduct electricity well as solids","They have low melting points","They are soft and easy to bend","They conduct electricity when molten or dissolved in water, but not as solids","They never conduct electricity, even when molten"],3,"In the solid the ions are locked in place, so there are no mobile charges and no conduction. Melting or dissolving frees the ions to move, so the liquid or the solution conducts. Ionic solids have high melting points and are hard but brittle.",["What must be able to move for electricity to flow?","Are the ions free to move in the solid?","Ions move only when the lattice is broken up."]],
["m8-a-040",8,15,2,"Concept","mcq","Why are ionic crystals hard but brittle?",["The ions are held by weak forces that break easily","A strong blow shifts one layer of ions so that like charges line up, and the repulsion splits the crystal","Free electrons move through the crystal and weaken it","The covalent bonds inside each ion break under stress","Ionic crystals contain molecules that slide past each other"],1,"The attractions in the lattice are strong (hard), but if a force moves one layer by one ion, cations face cations and anions face anions. The like-charge repulsions push the layers apart and the crystal cracks (brittle). The forces are not weak; ionic solids have no free electrons and no discrete molecules.",["Picture one row of + − + − sliding along the row below it.","What happens when like charges end up side by side?","Repulsion between like charges."]],
["m8-a-041",8,15,2,"Trap","mcq","Which statement about solid sodium chloride is FALSE?",["It has a high melting point (801 °C)","Its ions are held in fixed positions in a lattice","Its aqueous solution conducts electricity","It cracks rather than bends when struck hard","It conducts electricity well because it is made of charged ions"],4,"Solid NaCl does NOT conduct: the ions are held in fixed positions and cannot move. The other statements are true: high melting point from strong ionic attractions, fixed lattice positions, conducting solution (mobile ions), and brittleness.",["Containing ions is not enough for conduction.","The charges must be free to move.","Are the ions in a solid free to move?"]],
["m8-a-042",8,15,2,"Concept","mcq","Why do ionic compounds have high melting points?",["Ionic compounds contain strong covalent bonds that must be broken","The ions are very heavy","Each ion is held by strong electrostatic attractions to many oppositely charged neighbours, so much energy is needed to free the ions","Melting requires breaking the bonds inside polyatomic ions","Formula units are held together by hydrogen bonds"],2,"In a lattice every ion is surrounded by several ions of opposite charge; melting means disrupting all these strong Coulomb attractions, which takes a lot of energy (high melting point). There are no covalent bonds between Na⁺ and Cl⁻, mass is not the cause, polyatomic ions stay intact on melting, and hydrogen bonding is not involved.",["What force holds the ions in place?","Each ion attracts many neighbours, not just one.","Strong Coulomb attractions throughout the lattice."]],
["m8-a-043",8,15,3,"Exam","mcq","NaF melts at 993 °C. What would you predict for MgO, and why?",["Lower, because O is less electronegative than F","About the same, because the ions are nearly the same size","Lower, because Mg atoms are smaller than Na atoms","Much higher (it melts near 2850 °C), because the 2+ and 2− charges give a much larger lattice energy","Much higher, because MgO is covalent while NaF is ionic"],3,"Both are ionic with similar interionic distances, but MgO's charge product is 4 versus 1, so its lattice energy (≈ 3900 vs ≈ 923 kJ/mol) and melting point are much higher. Similar size does not mean similar strength when the charges differ; electronegativity does not set the melting point; MgO is ionic, not covalent.",["Compare charges and sizes of the ions.","Lattice energy controls the melting point of an ionic solid.","Charge product 4 versus 1."]],
["m8-a-044",8,15,1,"Concept","mcq","Which substance is best described as covalent (made of molecules)?",["SCl₂","CsF","CaO","Na₂S","MgBr₂"],0,"S and Cl are both nonmetals, so they share electrons and form SCl₂ molecules. CsF, CaO, Na₂S and MgBr₂ each combine a metal with a nonmetal and are ionic.",["Classify each element as a metal or a nonmetal.","Covalent = nonmetal + nonmetal.","Find the compound with no metal in it."]],
["m8-a-045",8,15,3,"Exam","mcq","Which choice correctly gives the bonding in KCl, Cl₂, HCl and K, in that order?",["ionic; polar covalent; nonpolar covalent; metallic","ionic; nonpolar covalent; polar covalent; metallic","polar covalent; nonpolar covalent; ionic; metallic","ionic; ionic; polar covalent; metallic","metallic; nonpolar covalent; polar covalent; ionic"],1,"KCl: metal + nonmetal → ionic. Cl₂: two identical atoms share electrons equally → nonpolar covalent. HCl: two different nonmetals, Cl more electronegative → polar covalent. K: a pure metal → metallic. Swapping Cl₂ and HCl, or calling HCl ionic, are the usual slips.",["Start with which elements are metals.","Identical atoms share equally; different nonmetals share unequally.","Metal+nonmetal, same atoms, different nonmetals, pure metal."]],
["m8-a-046",8,15,2,"Concept","mcq","When lithium and oxygen atoms form Li₂O, what happens in terms of Lewis symbols?",["One Li atom gives two electrons to O, which ends up with 8 dots","Each Li atom shares one pair with O, giving the molecule Li−O−Li","O gives one electron to each Li atom, so each Li ends up with 2 dots","Each of two Li atoms gives one electron to O, which ends up with 7 dots as O⁻","Each of two Li atoms gives its single valence electron to one O atom, which ends up with 8 dots as O²⁻"],4,"Li has 1 valence electron; O has 6 and needs 2 more. Two Li atoms each transfer one electron: 2 Li⁺ and an O²⁻ with an octet (8 dots), formula Li₂O. Li has only one valence electron to give; Li and O form an ionic (not covalent) compound; electrons move from the metal to the nonmetal; and two transferred electrons give O 8 dots and a 2− charge, not 7 and 1−.",["How many valence electrons does each atom start with?","O needs two electrons; each Li can give one.","6 + 2 = 8 dots, charge 2−."]],
["m8-a-047",8,15,2,"Concept","mcq","Which element would most likely form a 3− ion in an ionic compound with a reactive metal?",["Al","Cl","S","P","Na"],3,"P (group 5A) has 5 valence electrons and gains 3 to reach the argon configuration, forming P³⁻ (as in Na₃P). Al and Na are metals that form cations (Al³⁺, Na⁺); Cl gains 1 (Cl⁻); S gains 2 (S²⁻).",["Nonmetals gain electrons until they reach an octet.","Electrons gained = 8 − valence electrons.","Group 5A needs 3."]],
["m8-a-048",8,15,3,"Exam","mcq","Which ionic compound would you expect to have the LARGEST lattice energy?",["CaO","MgO","BaS","NaF","LiCl"],1,"Charge first: MgO, CaO and BaS are 2+/2− (charge product 4); NaF and LiCl are 1+/1− (product 1). Among the 2+/2− compounds, MgO has the smallest ions (Mg²⁺ and O²⁻), so the shortest distance and the largest lattice energy (≈ 3900 kJ/mol vs ≈ 3400 for CaO; BaS has two large ions).",["Highest charges first.","Then the smallest ions.","Small 2+ cation with small 2− anion."]],
["m8-a-049",8,15,4,"Trap","mcq","A student argues: 'KF has a larger lattice energy than LiF, because the electronegativity difference in KF is larger.' What is wrong with the argument?",["Nothing; ΔEN controls lattice energy, and KF has the larger ΔEN","KF's lattice energy is smaller, because K⁺ has a smaller charge than Li⁺","Lattice energy depends on ion charge and size, not ΔEN; K⁺ is larger than Li⁺, so KF's lattice energy is SMALLER (≈ 820 vs ≈ 1050 kJ/mol)","The two are equal, because the anion is the same","LiF is covalent, so it has no lattice energy"],2,"Coulomb's law: lattice energy ∝ q₁q₂/d. K⁺ and Li⁺ have the same 1+ charge, but K⁺ is much larger, so the K⁺−F⁻ distance is longer and KF's lattice energy is smaller. ΔEN tells you how ionic a bond is, not how strongly the lattice holds together. Both cations are 1+; the cations differ, so the values are not equal; LiF is ionic.",["What two things appear in Coulomb's law?","Compare the charges, then the sizes, of Li⁺ and K⁺.","Same charge; K⁺ is bigger."]],
["m8-a-050",8,15,3,"Exam","mcq","Which quantity is NOT needed to calculate the lattice energy of CaO with a Born-Haber cycle?",["The electron affinity of calcium","The second ionization energy of calcium","The O=O bond energy","The second electron affinity of oxygen","The standard enthalpy of formation of CaO"],0,"The cycle for CaO: Ca(s) → Ca(g); Ca(g) → Ca²⁺(g) (IE₁ + IE₂); ½O₂ → O(g) (½ × O=O bond energy); O(g) → O²⁻(g) (EA₁ + EA₂); ions → CaO(s); and ΔH°f to close the cycle. Calcium loses electrons, so its electron affinity never appears.",["List the steps that turn Ca(s) and O₂(g) into Ca²⁺(g) and O²⁻(g).","Ca loses electrons; O gains them.","Which atom never gains an electron?"]],
["m8-a-051",8,15,4,"Exam","mcq","Substance X is shiny, conducts electricity as a solid and as a liquid, and can be drawn into wire. Substance Y is hard and brittle, melts at 1100 °C, and conducts only when molten. Substance Z is a gas at room temperature that does not conduct in any state. Which bonding models fit X, Y and Z?",["metallic; ionic; covalent","ionic; metallic; covalent","metallic; covalent; ionic","covalent; ionic; metallic","ionic; covalent; metallic"],0,"X: conducts as a solid and is ductile → mobile electron sea → metallic. Y: high melting point, brittle, conducts only when the ions are freed by melting → ionic. Z: low boiling point and nonconducting → small covalent molecules held by weak forces between them.",["Which model allows conduction in the SOLID?","Brittle + conducts only when molten = ions locked in a lattice.","A nonconducting gas is made of molecules."]],
["m8-a-052",8,16,1,"Lecture","mcq","Bond order is:",["the total number of bonds an atom forms","the number of lone pairs on the two bonded atoms","the energy needed to break the bond","the number of electron pairs shared between two bonded atoms","the number of atoms bonded to a central atom"],3,"Bond order counts shared pairs between ONE pair of atoms: single = 1, double = 2, triple = 3 (and resonance can give averages such as 1.5). It is not the atom's total number of bonds, not lone pairs, and not an energy.",["It describes one bond between two particular atoms.","Single, double, triple ...","Count the shared pairs between the two atoms."]],
["m8-a-053",8,16,1,"Concept","num","What is the bond order of the nitrogen-nitrogen bond in N₂? (Enter a number.)",["3"],3,"N₂ has 10 valence electrons: :N≡N:, a triple bond (three shared pairs) with one lone pair on each N. Bond order = 3.",["Draw the Lewis structure of N₂ (10 valence electrons).","Each N needs an octet with only one partner.","Three shared pairs."],{"abs":0}],
["m8-a-054",8,16,2,"Concept","mcq","Arrange the carbon-carbon bonds in order of INCREASING bond length: C=C, C≡C, C−C.",["C−C < C=C < C≡C","C=C < C≡C < C−C","C≡C < C−C < C=C","C−C = C=C = C≡C","C≡C < C=C < C−C"],4,"More shared pairs pull the nuclei closer: higher bond order → shorter bond. C≡C ≈ 121 pm < C=C ≈ 134 pm < C−C ≈ 154 pm. 'C−C < C=C < C≡C' is the order of increasing bond ENERGY with the length idea reversed.",["How does bond order affect bond length?","Higher bond order = shorter bond.","Triple is shortest, single is longest."]],
["m8-a-055",8,16,2,"Concept","mcq","Arrange the nitrogen-nitrogen bonds in order of DECREASING bond energy: N−N, N=N, N≡N.",["N−N > N=N > N≡N","N=N > N≡N > N−N","N≡N > N=N > N−N","N≡N > N−N > N=N","N≡N = N=N = N−N, because each joins two N atoms"],2,"Higher bond order → stronger bond: N≡N (945 kJ/mol) > N=N (418) > N−N (160). Bonds between the same two elements are not equal in strength; the order of shared pairs matters.",["How does bond order affect bond strength?","Higher bond order = stronger bond.","Triple > double > single."]],
["m8-a-056",8,16,2,"Concept","mcq","Which carbon-oxygen or carbon-carbon bond is both the SHORTEST and the STRONGEST?",["C=O","C≡O","C−O","C−C","C=C"],1,"Bond order 3 between small C and O atoms: C≡O (as in carbon monoxide) is 113 pm and 1070 kJ/mol, shorter and stronger than C=O (123 pm, 745), C−O (143 pm, 358), C=C (134 pm, 614) and C−C (154 pm, 347).",["Look for the highest bond order.","Higher order → shorter and stronger.","The only triple bond in the list."]],
["m8-a-057",8,16,2,"Exam","mcq","Arrange in order of INCREASING bond length: H−I, H−F, H−Br, H−Cl.",["H−F < H−Cl < H−Br < H−I","H−I < H−Br < H−Cl < H−F","H−F < H−Br < H−Cl < H−I","H−Cl < H−F < H−I < H−Br","H−F < H−Cl < H−I < H−Br"],0,"All are single bonds, so atom size decides: halogen radius grows F < Cl < Br < I, so bond length grows H−F (92 pm) < H−Cl (127) < H−Br (141) < H−I (161). The reversed list is the order of decreasing length.",["All four are single bonds.","Bond length ≈ sum of the two atoms' radii.","Halogen atoms get bigger down the group."]],
["m8-a-058",8,16,3,"Exam","mcq","Arrange in order of DECREASING bond energy: H−Cl, H−I, H−F, H−Br.",["H−I > H−Br > H−Cl > H−F","H−Cl > H−F > H−Br > H−I","H−F > H−Cl > H−Br > H−I","H−F > H−Br > H−Cl > H−I","H−F > H−Cl > H−I > H−Br"],2,"For bonds of the same order, shorter bonds are stronger. Bond length rises F < Cl < Br < I, so bond energy falls: H−F (565 kJ/mol) > H−Cl (427) > H−Br (363) > H−I (295). Listing them by increasing energy is the reversed trap.",["Same bond order - which are shortest?","Shorter bond → stronger bond.","Small F gives the strongest bond to H."]],
["m8-a-059",8,16,1,"Concept","mcq","Which statement about bond energy (BE) is correct?",["It is always negative, because bonds release energy when they break","It is always positive, because energy must be absorbed to break a bond","It is positive for strong bonds and negative for weak bonds","It is the energy released when one mole of a gaseous compound forms from its elements","It is larger for longer bonds between the same two atoms"],1,"Bond energy is the enthalpy change to BREAK one mole of a given bond in gaseous molecules - always endothermic, so always positive. Breaking bonds never releases energy; formation from elements is ΔH°f; and for the same atoms, longer bonds (lower order) are weaker.",["Does breaking a bond take or give energy?","Bond breaking is endothermic.","BE is defined for breaking, so its sign is always +."]],
["m8-a-060",8,16,1,"Concept","mcq","Which relationship is correct for bonds between the same pair of atoms?",["Higher bond order → longer bond → greater bond energy","Higher bond order → shorter bond → smaller bond energy","Bond order affects bond energy but not bond length","Higher bond order → longer bond → smaller bond energy","Higher bond order → shorter bond → greater bond energy"],4,"More shared electron pairs pull the nuclei closer together (shorter) and hold them more tightly (stronger). Example: C−C 154 pm, 347 kJ/mol; C=C 134 pm, 614; C≡C 121 pm, 839.",["Think of C−C, C=C, C≡C.","More shared pairs pull the atoms closer.","Shorter bonds are stronger."]],
["m8-a-061",8,16,4,"Trap","mcq","C−C bond energy = 347 kJ/mol and C=C bond energy = 614 kJ/mol. Which conclusion is correct?",["A C=C bond is exactly twice as strong as a C−C bond","A C=C bond is more than twice as strong as a C−C bond","A C=C bond is longer than a C−C bond, which is why it is not twice as strong","A C=C bond is stronger than a C−C bond but less than twice as strong, so the second shared pair adds less energy than the first","A C=C bond is weaker than a C−C bond because the double bond strains the atoms"],3,"Twice a single bond would be 2 × 347 = 694 kJ/mol, but C=C is only 614 kJ/mol: stronger than C−C, yet less than double. The second shared pair contributes 614 − 347 = 267 kJ/mol, less than the first. C=C is shorter (134 pm) than C−C (154 pm), not longer.",["Compute 2 × 347.","Compare 694 with 614.","Stronger, but less than double."]],
["m8-a-062",8,16,2,"Calculation","num","The Cl−Cl bond in Cl₂ is 199 pm long and the C−C bond in ethane is 154 pm long. Using covalent radii, estimate the length of a C−Cl bond. (Enter a number in pm.)",["177 pm"],176.5,"Covalent radius = half the distance between identical bonded nuclei: r(Cl) = 199/2 = 99.5 pm; r(C) = 154/2 = 77 pm. Bond length ≈ r(C) + r(Cl) = 77 + 99.5 = 176.5 ≈ 177 pm (the measured C−Cl length is about 177 pm). Adding the two full bond lengths, or averaging them, are the usual slips.",["Get each atom's covalent radius from its homonuclear bond.","Radius = half of the X−X bond length.","154/2 + 199/2."]],
["m8-a-063",8,16,2,"Concept","mcq","Why does the bond length increase from H−F to H−Cl to H−Br to H−I?",["The bond order decreases from 1 in HF to less than 1 in HI","Electronegativity increases down the group","H−I contains more lone pairs than H−F","Heavier halogens form double bonds with hydrogen","The halogen atom gets larger down the group, so the distance between the bonded nuclei increases"],4,"Each is a single bond (bond order 1). Down group 7A each halogen has one more electron shell, so its covalent radius grows and the H−X distance grows: 92, 127, 141, 161 pm. Electronegativity decreases down the group; each halogen has three lone pairs in H−X; there are no H=X double bonds.",["Is the bond order the same in all four?","Bond length depends on atomic radius.","Atomic radius increases down a group."]],
["m8-a-064",8,16,4,"Trap","mcq","Covalent bonds are strong, yet substances made of small covalent molecules (such as CH₄ or I₂) melt and boil at low temperatures. Why?",["The covalent bonds in these molecules are weaker than ionic bonds, so they break on melting","Melting breaks about half of the covalent bonds in the sample","There are no attractions of any kind between covalent molecules","Melting and boiling separate whole molecules from each other, overcoming only the weak forces BETWEEN molecules; the covalent bonds stay intact","These molecules contain metallic bonds, which melt easily"],3,"When CH₄ boils, CH₄ molecules move apart; no C−H bond breaks. The forces between molecules (intermolecular forces) are much weaker than covalent bonds, so little energy is needed. Some attraction must exist between molecules, or they would never condense at all. No covalent or metallic bonds break.",["What actually separates when CH₄ boils - atoms or molecules?","Distinguish bonds within molecules from forces between molecules.","The C−H bonds are still there in the gas."]],
["m8-a-065",8,16,2,"Concept","mcq","Diamond and quartz (SiO₂) are covalent substances, but they melt at very high temperatures (quartz above 1600 °C). Why?",["They are network covalent solids: covalent bonds link every atom to its neighbours, so melting requires breaking covalent bonds","They are ionic compounds","They are held together by an electron sea","Their small molecules are very heavy","They contain hydrogen bonds between molecules"],0,"In a network covalent solid there are no separate molecules; the whole crystal is one covalently bonded network, so melting means breaking strong covalent bonds. C and SiO₂ are not ionic or metallic, they are not made of small molecules, and they contain no O−H or N−H for hydrogen bonding.",["Are there separate molecules in diamond?","Every atom is covalently bonded into one network.","Melting requires breaking covalent bonds."]],
["m8-a-066",8,16,2,"Concept","mcq","Which substance would you expect to have the LOWEST melting point?",["NaCl","MgO","CH₄","Cu","SiO₂ (quartz)"],2,"CH₄ is made of small nonpolar molecules held by weak forces between molecules; it melts at −182 °C. NaCl and MgO are ionic (high melting), Cu is metallic (1085 °C), and SiO₂ is a network covalent solid (above 1600 °C).",["Which substance is made of small separate molecules?","Melting molecules apart overcomes only weak forces.","The only molecular substance in the list."]],
["m8-a-067",8,16,1,"Concept","mcq","Which statement about energy and bonds is true?",["Breaking a bond always releases energy, and forming a bond always absorbs energy","Breaking a bond always absorbs energy, and forming a bond always releases energy","Breaking strong bonds releases energy, but breaking weak bonds absorbs energy","Both breaking and forming bonds release energy","Forming a bond absorbs energy only when the bond is polar"],1,"Atoms in a bond are at lower energy than separated atoms, so pulling them apart costs energy (endothermic, +BE) and letting them bond releases the same energy (exothermic, −BE). This is true for every bond, strong or weak, polar or not.",["Are bonded atoms at higher or lower energy than separate atoms?","Going up in energy absorbs energy.","Breaking = endothermic; forming = exothermic."]],
["m8-a-068",8,16,2,"Trap","mcq","Which expression estimates ΔH°rxn from average bond energies?",["ΔH°rxn ≈ ΣBE(bonds formed in products) − ΣBE(bonds broken in reactants)","ΔH°rxn ≈ ΣBE(reactant bonds) + ΣBE(product bonds)","ΔH°rxn ≈ ΣnΔH°f(products) − ΣBE(reactant bonds)","ΔH°rxn ≈ −ΣBE(bonds broken in reactants)","ΔH°rxn ≈ ΣBE(bonds broken in reactants) − ΣBE(bonds formed in products)"],4,"Breaking reactant bonds absorbs +ΣBE(broken); forming product bonds releases −ΣBE(formed). Adding: ΔH ≈ ΣBE(broken) − ΣBE(formed), i.e. reactants minus products. This is the REVERSE of the ΔH°f formula (products − reactants), which is exactly the trap in the first distractor. Bond energies and ΔH°f values are never mixed in one formula.",["Breaking costs energy (+); forming releases energy (−).","Add the two contributions.","Reactant bonds minus product bonds - opposite to the ΔH°f formula."]],
["m8-a-069",8,16,2,"Concept","mcq","According to bond energies, a reaction is exothermic when:",["the bonds broken in the reactants are, in total, stronger than the bonds formed","the bonds formed in the products are, in total, stronger than the bonds broken in the reactants","more bonds are broken than are formed","the products contain more double bonds than the reactants","all reactants and products are gases"],1,"ΔH ≈ ΣBE(broken) − ΣBE(formed) is negative when ΣBE(formed) is larger - when weaker bonds are traded for stronger ones. The number of bonds, the number of double bonds, or the phase alone do not decide it.",["Use ΔH ≈ ΣBE(broken) − ΣBE(formed).","When is that difference negative?","More energy released (formed) than absorbed (broken)."]],
["m8-a-070",8,16,2,"Calculation","mcq","Estimate ΔH°rxn for H₂(g) + Cl₂(g) → 2HCl(g). Bond energies (kJ/mol): H−H 432, Cl−Cl 243, H−Cl 427.",["−179 kJ","+179 kJ","+248 kJ","−422 kJ","−1529 kJ"],0,"Broken: 1 H−H + 1 Cl−Cl = 432 + 243 = 675 kJ. Formed: 2 H−Cl = 2 × 427 = 854 kJ. ΔH ≈ 675 − 854 = −179 kJ. +179 is formed − broken (wrong order); +248 counts only one H−Cl; −422 forgets to break Cl−Cl; −1529 adds every bond energy with a minus sign.",["List the bonds broken and the bonds formed.","Two H−Cl bonds form.","(432 + 243) − 2(427)."]],
["m8-a-071",8,16,2,"Calculation","mcq","Estimate ΔH°rxn for H₂(g) + F₂(g) → 2HF(g). Bond energies (kJ/mol): H−H 432, F−F 159, H−F 565.",["+539 kJ","+26 kJ","−539 kJ","−26 kJ","−1721 kJ"],2,"Broken: H−H + F−F = 432 + 159 = 591 kJ. Formed: 2 H−F = 1130 kJ. ΔH ≈ 591 − 1130 = −539 kJ. +539 subtracts in the wrong order; ±26 forget the coefficient 2 on HF; −1721 adds all bond energies.",["Count bonds in 2HF, not 1.","ΔH ≈ ΣBE(broken) − ΣBE(formed).","(432 + 159) − 2(565)."]],
["m8-a-072",8,16,3,"Calculation","num","Estimate ΔH°rxn for N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) → 2NH₃(g) using bond energies (kJ/mol): N≡N 945, H−H 432, N−H 391. (Enter a number in kJ, with its sign.)",["−105 kJ"],-105,"Broken: 1 N≡N + 3 H−H = 945 + 3(432) = 945 + 1296 = 2241 kJ. Formed: 2 NH₃ × 3 N−H each = 6 N−H = 6(391) = 2346 kJ. ΔH ≈ 2241 − 2346 = −105 kJ. Common slips: using 3 N−H instead of 6 (gives +1068), or products − reactants (+105).",["Each NH₃ has three N−H bonds - and there are two NH₃.","Broken: N≡N + 3 H−H; formed: 6 N−H.","2241 − 2346."],{"tol":0.01}],
["m8-a-073",8,16,1,"Concept","num","When one CH₄ molecule burns completely (CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O), how many C−H bonds are broken? (Enter a whole number.)",["4"],4,"Methane is a C atom single-bonded to four H atoms; in combustion every C−H bond is broken (the carbon ends up in CO₂ and all hydrogens in H₂O), so 4 C−H bonds are broken per CH₄. Counting only one C−H is a classic bond-energy error.",["Draw CH₄.","Is any C−H bond left in the products?","Carbon has four H atoms attached."],{"abs":0}],
["m8-a-074",8,16,4,"Calculation","mcq","Estimate ΔH°rxn for CH₄(g) + 2O₂(g) → CO₂(g) + 2H₂O(g). Bond energies (kJ/mol): C−H 413, O=O 498, C=O (in CO₂) 799, O−H 467.",["+818 kJ","+116 kJ","−2057 kJ","−818 kJ","−710 kJ"],3,"Broken: 4 C−H + 2 O=O = 4(413) + 2(498) = 1652 + 996 = 2648 kJ. Formed: 2 C=O (one CO₂) + 4 O−H (two H₂O) = 2(799) + 4(467) = 1598 + 1868 = 3466 kJ. ΔH ≈ 2648 − 3466 = −818 kJ. +818 is formed − broken; +116 counts O−H bonds for only one H₂O; −2057 breaks only one C−H; −710 uses the generic C=O value 745 instead of the CO₂ value given.",["CH₄ has 4 C−H bonds; CO₂ has 2 C=O; each H₂O has 2 O−H.","Broken = 4(413) + 2(498); formed = 2(799) + 4(467).","2648 − 3466."]],
["m8-a-075",8,16,3,"Calculation","num","Estimate ΔH°rxn for 2H₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2H₂O(g) using bond energies (kJ/mol): H−H 432, O=O 498, O−H 467. (Enter a number in kJ, with its sign.)",["−506 kJ"],-506,"Broken: 2 H−H + 1 O=O = 864 + 498 = 1362 kJ. Formed: 2 H₂O × 2 O−H = 4 O−H = 1868 kJ. ΔH ≈ 1362 − 1868 = −506 kJ (for the equation as written, i.e. per 2 mol H₂O).",["Two water molecules each have two O−H bonds.","Broken: 2(432) + 498. Formed: 4(467).","1362 − 1868."],{"tol":0.01}],
["m8-a-076",8,16,3,"Trap","mcq","Estimate ΔH°rxn for C₂H₄(g) + H₂(g) → C₂H₆(g) (ethylene + hydrogen → ethane). Bond energies (kJ/mol): C−H 413, C−C 347, C=C 614, H−H 432.",["−127 kJ","+127 kJ","−394 kJ","−559 kJ","−1173 kJ"],0,"Ethylene H₂C=CH₂ has 4 C−H + 1 C=C; ethane has 6 C−H + 1 C−C. Broken: 4(413) + 614 + 432 = 2698 kJ. Formed: 6(413) + 347 = 2825 kJ. ΔH ≈ 2698 − 2825 = −127 kJ. (Shortcut: only C=C and H−H break, only C−C and 2 C−H form: 1046 − 1173 = −127.) −394 treats the C=C as a C−C single bond; −559 forgets to break H−H; −1173 counts only the bonds formed; +127 subtracts in the wrong order.",["Draw ethylene: the two carbons share a double bond.","Broken: 4 C−H, 1 C=C, 1 H−H. Formed: 6 C−H, 1 C−C.","2698 − 2825."]],
["m8-a-077",8,16,3,"Calculation","mcq","Estimate ΔH°rxn for CH₄(g) + Cl₂(g) → CH₃Cl(g) + HCl(g). Bond energies (kJ/mol): C−H 413, Cl−Cl 243, C−Cl 339, H−Cl 427.",["+110 kJ","+317 kJ","−523 kJ","−766 kJ","−110 kJ"],4,"Broken: 4 C−H + Cl−Cl = 1652 + 243 = 1895 kJ. Formed: 3 C−H + C−Cl + H−Cl = 1239 + 339 + 427 = 2005 kJ. ΔH ≈ 1895 − 2005 = −110 kJ (net: one C−H and one Cl−Cl broken, one C−Cl and one H−Cl formed: 656 − 766). +317 forgets the H−Cl bond formed; −523 gives CH₃Cl four C−H bonds; −766 counts only bonds formed; +110 has the wrong order.",["CH₃Cl keeps three of methane's C−H bonds.","Net change: break C−H and Cl−Cl; form C−Cl and H−Cl.","(413 + 243) − (339 + 427)."]],
["m8-a-078",8,16,4,"Exam","mcq","Estimate ΔH°rxn for the combustion of propane: C₃H₈(g) + 5O₂(g) → 3CO₂(g) + 4H₂O(g). Bond energies (kJ/mol): C−C 347, C−H 413, O=O 498, C=O (in CO₂) 799, O−H 467.",["+2042 kJ","−2736 kJ","+355 kJ","−2042 kJ","−174 kJ"],3,"Propane CH₃−CH₂−CH₃ has 2 C−C and 8 C−H bonds. Broken: 2(347) + 8(413) + 5(498) = 694 + 3304 + 2490 = 6488 kJ. Formed: 3 CO₂ × 2 C=O = 6(799) = 4794; 4 H₂O × 2 O−H = 8(467) = 3736; total 8530 kJ. ΔH ≈ 6488 − 8530 = −2042 kJ. −2736 forgets the two C−C bonds; +355 counts one C=O per CO₂; −174 counts one O−H per H₂O; +2042 is formed − broken.",["Draw propane's carbon chain and count C−C and C−H bonds.","Each CO₂ has 2 C=O; each H₂O has 2 O−H.","6488 − 8530."]],
["m8-a-079",8,16,4,"Exam","mcq","Estimate ΔH per mole of methanol burned: CH₃OH(g) + 3/2 O₂(g) → CO₂(g) + 2H₂O(g). Bond energies (kJ/mol): C−H 413, C−O 358, O−H 467, O=O 498, C=O (in CO₂) 799.",["−1122 kJ","−1013 kJ","−655 kJ","+655 kJ","−1310 kJ"],2,"Methanol H₃C−O−H has 3 C−H, 1 C−O and 1 O−H. Broken: 3(413) + 358 + 467 + 1.5(498) = 1239 + 358 + 467 + 747 = 2811 kJ. Formed: 2 C=O + 4 O−H = 1598 + 1868 = 3466 kJ. ΔH ≈ 2811 − 3466 = −655 kJ per mol CH₃OH. −1122 forgets methanol's own O−H bond; −1013 forgets the C−O bond; −1310 is for 2 mol (the equation doubled); +655 has the wrong order.",["Draw methanol and list every bond, including the O−H.","Broken: 3 C−H, C−O, O−H, 1.5 O=O. Formed: 2 C=O, 4 O−H.","2811 − 3466."]],
["m8-a-080",8,16,4,"Trap","mcq","Estimate ΔH°rxn for C₂H₂(g) + 2H₂(g) → C₂H₆(g) (acetylene → ethane). Bond energies (kJ/mol): C−H 413, C≡C 839, C−C 347, H−H 432.",["+296 kJ","−296 kJ","−728 kJ","−94 kJ","−521 kJ"],1,"Acetylene H−C≡C−H: 2 C−H + 1 C≡C. Ethane: 6 C−H + 1 C−C. Broken: 2(413) + 839 + 2(432) = 826 + 839 + 864 = 2529 kJ. Formed: 6(413) + 347 = 2825 kJ. ΔH ≈ 2529 − 2825 = −296 kJ. −728 breaks only one H−H; −94 treats C≡C as three single C−C bonds (3 × 347); −521 uses the C=C value (614) for the triple bond; +296 has the wrong order.",["Two moles of H₂ are consumed.","Use the triple-bond energy given for C≡C.","(826 + 839 + 864) − (2478 + 347)."]],
["m8-a-081",8,16,4,"Calculation","num","For H₂(g) + Br₂(g) → 2HBr(g), ΔH°rxn = −103 kJ. Given bond energies H−H = 432 kJ/mol and Br−Br = 193 kJ/mol, estimate the H−Br bond energy. (Enter a number in kJ/mol.)",["364 kJ/mol"],364,"ΔH ≈ ΣBE(broken) − ΣBE(formed): −103 = (432 + 193) − 2·BE(H−Br). So 2·BE(H−Br) = 625 + 103 = 728 and BE(H−Br) = 364 kJ/mol (tables list ≈ 363). Forgetting the 2 gives 728; getting the sign of ΔH backwards gives (625 − 103)/2 = 261.",["Set up ΔH ≈ ΣBE(broken) − ΣBE(formed) with H−Br as the unknown.","Two H−Br bonds form: −103 = 625 − 2x.","x = (625 + 103)/2."],{"tol":0.01}],
["m8-a-082",8,16,4,"Calculation","mcq","For 2H₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2H₂O(g), ΔH° = −483.6 kJ. Using H−H = 432 kJ/mol and O=O = 498 kJ/mol, estimate the average O−H bond energy.",["923 kJ/mol","220 kJ/mol","461 kJ/mol","353 kJ/mol","337 kJ/mol"],2,"−483.6 = [2(432) + 498] − 4·BE(O−H), because two H₂O molecules contain 4 O−H bonds. 4·BE(O−H) = 1362 + 483.6 = 1845.6 → BE(O−H) = 461.4 ≈ 461 kJ/mol. 923 divides by 2 (one O−H per H₂O); 220 uses +483.6; 353 breaks only one H−H; 337 forgets the O=O bond.",["How many O−H bonds are in 2H₂O?","−483.6 = 1362 − 4x.","x = (1362 + 483.6)/4."]],
["m8-a-083",8,16,3,"Calculation","mcq","Use standard enthalpies of formation to find ΔH°rxn for CH₄(g) + 2O₂(g) → CO₂(g) + 2H₂O(g). ΔH°f (kJ/mol): CH₄(g) −74.9; CO₂(g) −393.5; H₂O(g) −241.8; H₂O(l) −285.8.",["−802.2 kJ","−952.0 kJ","−890.2 kJ","−560.4 kJ","+802.2 kJ"],0,"ΔH° = ΣnΔH°f(products) − ΣnΔH°f(reactants) = [−393.5 + 2(−241.8)] − [−74.9 + 0] = −877.1 + 74.9 = −802.2 kJ. −952.0 adds CH₄'s value instead of subtracting it; −890.2 uses liquid water (the equation says H₂O(g)); −560.4 forgets the coefficient 2; +802.2 is reactants − products. Note the ΔH°f formula is products − reactants, while the bond-energy formula is broken (reactants) − formed (products).",["Products minus reactants for ΔH°f; O₂ is an element (0).","Use the H₂O(g) value, times 2.","[−393.5 + 2(−241.8)] − (−74.9)."]],
["m8-a-084",8,16,4,"Concept","mcq","For CH₄(g) + 2O₂(g) → CO₂(g) + 2H₂O(g), average bond energies give ΔH ≈ −818 kJ, while ΔH°f values give −802.2 kJ. What is the main reason for the difference?",["The bond-energy method gives ΔH per mole of O₂, not per mole of CH₄","Tabulated bond energies are averages over many different molecules, so the bond-energy method is only an estimate","ΔH°f values are for liquids, while bond energies are for gases","One of the two methods must have used the wrong sign convention","Bond energies apply only to ionic compounds"],1,"A C−H or O−H bond energy in a table is an AVERAGE over many compounds; the actual bond strength in a particular molecule differs a little, so the sum is approximate (here within about 2%). ΔH°f values come from measurements on the actual substances, so the ΔH°f result is the accurate one. Both results are per mole of CH₄ with gaseous water, and both signs are right.",["The two answers are close but not equal - why only close?","A bond's energy varies a little from molecule to molecule.","Average bond energies."]],
["m8-a-085",8,16,4,"Trap","mcq","A student estimates ΔH for H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl by calculating 2(427) − (432 + 243) = +179 kJ and concludes the reaction is endothermic. (H−H 432, Cl−Cl 243, H−Cl 427 kJ/mol.) What is the error?",["Only one H−Cl bond should be counted, so ΔH = −248 kJ","There is no error: forming bonds absorbs energy, so +179 kJ is right","All the bond energies should be added: ΔH = +1529 kJ","The subtraction is in the wrong order: ΔH ≈ ΣBE(broken) − ΣBE(formed) = 675 − 854 = −179 kJ, so the reaction is exothermic","Only half the Cl−Cl energy should be used, so ΔH = +300.5 kJ"],3,"The student used products − reactants, the ΔH°f habit. With bond energies it is bonds broken (reactants) minus bonds formed (products): (432 + 243) − 2(427) = −179 kJ, exothermic, because the two H−Cl bonds formed are stronger in total than the bonds broken. Two HCl molecules form (two H−Cl bonds); forming bonds releases energy; and the whole Cl₂ molecule is broken.",["Which formula has products first: ΔH°f or bond energy?","ΔH ≈ ΣBE(broken) − ΣBE(formed).","675 − 854."]],
["m8-a-086",8,16,1,"Concept","mcq","Which element has the highest electronegativity?",["O","Cl","N","Cs","F"],4,"Electronegativity increases up a group and to the right across a period, so fluorine (top right, excluding noble gases) is the highest at 4.0. O is 3.5, N and Cl 3.0; Cs (bottom left) is among the lowest (0.7).",["Where in the table is electronegativity highest?","Up and to the right.","Top of group 7A."]],
["m8-a-087",8,16,1,"Lecture","mcq","Electronegativity (EN) is:",["the energy change when a gaseous atom gains an electron","the energy needed to remove an electron from a gaseous atom","the number of valence electrons in an atom","the relative ability of a bonded atom to attract the shared electrons in a bond","the charge an atom carries in an ionic compound"],3,"EN is a relative (unitless, Pauling-scale) measure of how strongly an atom in a bond pulls the shared pair toward itself. The energy change on gaining an electron is the electron affinity; removing one is the ionization energy - both are measured for isolated gaseous atoms.",["It describes an atom IN a bond.","It is relative, not a measured energy.","Pull on shared electrons."]],
["m8-a-088",8,16,2,"Concept","mcq","What is the general trend in electronegativity in the periodic table?",["It decreases from left to right across a period and increases down a group","It increases from left to right and increases down a group","It increases from left to right across a period and decreases down a group","It decreases from left to right and decreases down a group","It is roughly the same for all main-group elements"],2,"EN follows effective nuclear charge and size: across a period Z_eff rises and atoms shrink → EN rises; down a group atoms get larger and the shared electrons are farther from the nucleus → EN falls. So F is highest and Cs/Fr lowest.",["Think about where F and Cs sit.","Smaller atoms with higher Z_eff pull harder on shared electrons.","Up and to the right = higher."]],
["m8-a-089",8,16,2,"Exam","mcq","Without using a table, arrange in order of INCREASING electronegativity: O, Mg, K, C.",["O < C < Mg < K","K < Mg < C < O","Mg < K < C < O","K < Mg < O < C","K < C < Mg < O"],1,"EN rises to the right and up. K (period 4, group 1A) is lowest; Mg (period 3, group 2A) is higher; C and O are period 2 nonmetals with O farther right. Values: K 0.8 < Mg 1.2 < C 2.5 < O 3.5. The reversed list is the decreasing order.",["Locate each element: group and period.","Lower-left is lowest; upper-right is highest.","The group 1A metal in period 4 is lowest."]],
["m8-a-090",8,16,3,"Concept","mcq","Which statement correctly distinguishes electronegativity (EN) from electron affinity (EA)?",["EN describes an atom's pull on shared electrons in a bond (a relative, unitless scale); EA is the energy change when an isolated gaseous atom gains an electron","They are the same quantity measured in different units","EN is measured for isolated gaseous atoms, while EA applies only to bonded atoms","EN is always negative and EA is always positive","EA is a relative scale with no units, while EN is measured in kJ/mol"],0,"EN (Pauling scale, F = 4.0) is a relative number for atoms in bonds. EA is a measured energy (kJ/mol) for X(g) + e⁻ → X⁻(g), usually negative for nonmetals. They are related trends but different quantities; the other choices swap or invent their definitions and signs.",["One is for atoms in a bond; one is for lone gaseous atoms.","Which one has units of kJ/mol?","EN: in a bond, unitless. EA: gaseous atom, energy."]],
["m8-a-091",8,16,1,"Calculation","num","Using electronegativities O = 3.5 and H = 2.1, what is ΔEN for an O−H bond? (Enter a number.)",["1.4"],1.4,"ΔEN = |EN(O) − EN(H)| = 3.5 − 2.1 = 1.4. Between 0 and 1.7, so O−H is a polar covalent bond with O δ− and H δ+.",["ΔEN is a difference, not a sum.","Subtract the smaller EN from the larger.","3.5 − 2.1."],{"abs":0.01}],
["m8-a-092",8,16,2,"Concept","mcq","Which bond is the MOST polar? (EN: H 2.1, F 4.0, Cl 3.0, Br 2.8, I 2.5)",["H−Cl","H−Br","H−I","H−H","H−F"],4,"Bond polarity grows with ΔEN: H−F 1.9, H−Cl 0.9, H−Br 0.7, H−I 0.4, H−H 0. H−F is the most polar.",["Compute ΔEN for each bond.","Largest ΔEN = most polar.","4.0 − 2.1."]],
["m8-a-093",8,16,3,"Exam","mcq","Rank in order of INCREASING bond polarity: N−H, F−H, C−H, O−H. (EN: C 2.5, N 3.0, O 3.5, F 4.0, H 2.1)",["C−H < N−H < O−H < F−H","F−H < O−H < N−H < C−H","N−H < C−H < O−H < F−H","C−H < O−H < N−H < F−H","C−H < N−H < F−H < O−H"],0,"ΔEN values: C−H 0.4, N−H 0.9, O−H 1.4, F−H 1.9. Increasing polarity follows increasing ΔEN: C−H < N−H < O−H < F−H. The reverse list is decreasing polarity.",["Compute ΔEN for each bond.","Polarity rises with ΔEN.","0.4, 0.9, 1.4, 1.9."]],
["m8-a-094",8,16,3,"Trap","mcq","Which bond is nonpolar covalent? (EN: C 2.5, S 2.5, O 3.5, N 3.0, H 2.1, Cl 3.0, P 2.1)",["C−O","C−S","N−H","P−Cl","C−Cl"],1,"C and S both have EN 2.5, so ΔEN = 0 and the electrons are shared equally: nonpolar covalent even though the atoms are different. C−O (1.0), N−H (0.9), P−Cl (0.9) and C−Cl (0.5) are polar covalent. Trap: 'different atoms always make a polar bond' is not true when the ENs are equal.",["Compute ΔEN for each pair.","Nonpolar means ΔEN ≈ 0.","Two different elements can have the same EN."]],
["m8-a-095",8,16,3,"Trap","mcq","Which bond is the LEAST polar? (EN: N 3.0, Cl 3.0, H 2.1, O 3.5, C 2.5, F 4.0)",["N−H","N−O","C−N","Cl−F","N−Cl"],4,"ΔEN: N−Cl 0, N−H 0.9, N−O 0.5, C−N 0.5, Cl−F 1.0. N and Cl have the same Pauling EN (3.0), so the N−Cl bond is essentially nonpolar.",["Compute ΔEN for each bond.","The smallest ΔEN is the least polar.","Look for two atoms with EN 3.0."]],
["m8-a-096",8,16,2,"Concept","mcq","In a C−Cl bond (EN: C 2.5, Cl 3.0), which describes the partial charges?",["C is δ− and Cl is δ+","Both atoms are δ−","C is δ+ and Cl is δ−","C is +1 and Cl is −1","There are no partial charges, because both atoms are nonmetals"],2,"The more electronegative atom (Cl, 3.0) pulls the shared pair closer and gets a partial negative charge, δ−; C (2.5) is left δ+. These are partial (fractional) charges, not full ionic +1/−1, and bonds between two different nonmetals are usually polar.",["Which atom pulls harder on the shared pair?","The more electronegative atom becomes δ−.","Cl 3.0 vs C 2.5."]],
["m8-a-097",8,16,3,"Trap","mcq","In the C−Mg bond of a Grignard reagent (EN: C 2.5, Mg 1.2), which atom carries the partial NEGATIVE charge?",["Mg, because metals always carry δ−","Mg, because it has more electrons than C","Neither; a C−Mg bond is nonpolar","C, because it is more electronegative than Mg","C, because carbon is δ− in every bond it forms"],3,"δ− goes to the more electronegative atom. C (2.5) is more electronegative than Mg (1.2), so C is δ− and Mg δ+ (ΔEN = 1.3, polar). Metals are the δ+ end; and carbon is not always δ−: in C−Cl or C−O it is δ+.",["Compare the two EN values.","Higher EN → δ−.","2.5 vs 1.2."]],
["m8-a-098",8,16,2,"Exam","mcq","Using ΔEN (about 0 → nonpolar covalent; between 0 and about 1.7 → polar covalent; above about 1.7 → mostly ionic), classify the bonding in Cl₂, HCl and KCl. (EN: H 2.1, Cl 3.0, K 0.8)",["polar covalent; nonpolar covalent; ionic","nonpolar covalent; ionic; ionic","nonpolar covalent; polar covalent; polar covalent","nonpolar covalent; polar covalent; ionic","ionic; polar covalent; nonpolar covalent"],3,"Cl₂: ΔEN = 0 → nonpolar covalent. HCl: 3.0 − 2.1 = 0.9 → polar covalent. KCl: 3.0 − 0.8 = 2.2 → mostly ionic.",["Compute ΔEN for each.","0, 0.9, 2.2.","Compare each with 0 and 1.7."]],
["m8-a-099",8,16,3,"Exam","mcq","Which pair of elements forms the bond with the GREATEST ionic character? (EN: K 0.8, F 4.0, Ca 1.0, O 3.5, Na 0.9, Cl 3.0, Li 1.0, Br 2.8, Mg 1.2, S 2.5)",["Ca and O","K and F","Na and Cl","Li and Br","Mg and S"],1,"Ionic character grows with ΔEN: K−F 3.2, Ca−O 2.5, Na−Cl 2.1, Li−Br 1.8, Mg−S 1.3. KF has the largest ΔEN.",["Ionic character follows ΔEN.","Compute each difference.","4.0 − 0.8."]],
["m8-a-100",8,16,4,"Exam","mcq","Which molecule contains the MOST polar bond, and which atom in that bond is δ−? (EN: H 2.1, C 2.5, N 3.0, O 3.5, S 2.5, Cl 3.0)",["H₂O; O is δ−","NH₃; N is δ−","H₂S; S is δ−","HCl; H is δ−","CH₄; C is δ−"],0,"ΔEN: O−H 1.4, N−H 0.9, Cl−H 0.9, S−H 0.4, C−H 0.4. The O−H bond in water is the most polar, and O (higher EN) is δ−. In HCl the δ− atom would be Cl, not H.",["Find the bond with the largest ΔEN.","The δ− atom is the more electronegative one.","3.5 − 2.1."]],
["m8-a-101",8,16,2,"Concept","mcq","In Silberberg's polar-arrow notation, the arrow drawn along a polar bond:",["points toward the less electronegative atom","points from the smaller atom toward the larger atom","points toward whichever atom has more lone pairs, regardless of EN","is drawn only for ionic bonds","points toward the more electronegative (δ−) atom, with a small cross at its tail at the δ+ end"],4,"The polar arrow shows where the shared electrons are pulled: its head is at the δ− (more electronegative) atom and the crossed tail (looking like a + sign) is at the δ+ atom, e.g. H−F with the arrow pointing to F.",["Which way are the shared electrons pulled?","The tail looks like a plus sign.","The head points to δ−."]],
["m8-a-102",8,16,3,"Concept","mcq","Chlorine in HCl is assigned an oxidation number of −1. How does that assignment use electronegativity?",["The shared electrons are split equally between H and Cl","Both shared electrons are given to H because it is the smaller atom","Both shared electrons are counted as belonging to the more electronegative atom (Cl), as if the bond were ionic","Cl is −1 simply because it has seven valence electrons","The oxidation number is the formal charge, which is −1 for Cl in HCl"],2,"Oxidation numbers give ALL shared electrons to the more electronegative atom: Cl 'owns' 8 electrons vs 7 valence → −1; H owns 0 vs 1 → +1. Splitting shared electrons equally is the FORMAL-CHARGE bookkeeping, which gives 0 for both atoms in HCl.",["Oxidation numbers treat each bond as if it were ionic.","Who gets the shared pair?","The more electronegative atom takes both electrons."]],
["m8-a-103",8,16,3,"Concept","mcq","Bond polarity is described as a continuum. Which statement is correct?",["A bond is either 100% ionic or 100% covalent, with nothing in between","Bonds with ΔEN above 1.7 have no shared electron density at all","Every bond between atoms of different EN has some polarity; nonpolar, polar covalent and ionic are regions along a continuous scale of ΔEN","Any bond between two nonmetals is nonpolar","Any bond to a metal is ionic, whatever the ΔEN"],2,"Bond character changes smoothly with ΔEN: ΔEN 0 shares equally, small ΔEN gives partial charges, large ΔEN approaches full transfer. Even 'ionic' compounds keep some covalent character, many nonmetal−nonmetal bonds are polar, and metal-containing bonds with small ΔEN can be largely covalent. The 1.7 boundary is a rough guide, not a wall.",["Is there a sharp jump at ΔEN = 1.7?","Think of ΔEN as a dial, not a switch.","Continuous scale."]],
["m8-a-104",8,16,2,"Concept","mcq","Where are the partial charges in a water molecule? (EN: O 3.5, H 2.1)",["δ+ on O and δ− on each H","δ− on one H and δ+ on the other H","No partial charges, because H₂O is covalent","A full −2 charge on O and +1 on each H","δ− on O and δ+ on each H"],4,"O is more electronegative (3.5 vs 2.1), so each O−H bond's electrons are pulled toward O: O carries δ−, and each H carries δ+. These are partial charges in polar covalent bonds, not full ionic charges (the oxidation numbers −2/+1 are bookkeeping, not real charges).",["Which atom has the higher EN?","The more electronegative atom is δ−.","O pulls the shared pairs."]],
["m8-a-105",8,16,1,"Concept","mcq","Which best describes the electron-sea model of metallic bonding?",["Metal cations sit in a 'sea' of delocalized valence electrons that belong to the whole crystal","Anions are surrounded by a sea of protons","Localized electron pairs are shared between neighbouring metal atoms","Positive and negative ions are held in a lattice","Neutral atoms are held only by weak attractions between them"],0,"In a metal, each atom donates its valence electrons to a shared pool spread over the whole piece; the remaining cations are held together by their attraction to this mobile electron sea. Localized shared pairs are covalent bonding; a lattice of + and − ions is ionic bonding.",["Who owns the valence electrons in a metal?","Electrons are delocalized over the whole crystal.","Cations in an electron sea."]],
["m8-a-106",8,16,2,"Concept","mcq","Why do metals conduct electricity in the solid state?",["Metal cations move through the solid when a voltage is applied","Metals contain ions that become mobile only when melted","Metal atoms transfer electrons to impurities","Their delocalized valence electrons are free to move through the whole piece of metal","Metals have unusually high electronegativities"],3,"Conduction needs mobile charges. In a metal the valence electrons are delocalized, so they move freely even in the solid. The cations stay put; the 'conducts only when molten' description fits ionic compounds; metals have LOW electronegativities.",["What moving particles carry the current in a wire?","The electron sea is mobile.","Delocalized electrons."]],
["m8-a-107",8,16,3,"Concept","mcq","Why can metals be hammered into thin sheets, while ionic solids shatter?",["Metal atoms are held by weaker forces than ions, so they can bend","When layers of metal cations shift, they are still surrounded by the electron sea, so no strong repulsions develop; shifting an ionic lattice brings like charges together","Metals contain covalent bonds that can rotate","Ionic solids contain free electrons that make them brittle","Hammering melts the surface of a metal but not of an ionic solid"],1,"In a metal every cation is bathed in delocalized electrons; after a layer slides, the surroundings look the same, so the metal deforms instead of breaking (malleable, ductile). In an ionic solid, sliding a layer puts + next to + and − next to −, and the repulsion cracks the crystal. Metallic bonding is not necessarily weak (many metals melt above 1000 °C).",["Picture the surroundings of a cation before and after a layer slides.","In a metal, the electron sea moves with the cations.","No like-charge repulsions develop in a metal."]],
["m8-a-108",8,16,3,"Exam","mcq","Arrange in order of INCREASING melting point: Al, Na, Mg.",["Al < Mg < Na","Mg < Na < Al","Na < Al < Mg","Na < Mg < Al","Mg < Al < Na"],3,"Across period 3, each atom adds more valence electrons to the sea (Na 1, Mg 2, Al 3) and the cation gets smaller and more highly charged, so metallic bonding strengthens: Na 98 °C < Mg 649 °C < Al 660 °C.",["How many valence electrons does each metal give to the sea?","More valence electrons → stronger metallic bonding.","1, 2, 3 valence electrons."]],
["m8-a-109",8,16,3,"Exam","mcq","Which correctly ranks the melting points of the group 1A metals?",["Cs > Rb > K > Na > Li","Na > Li > K > Rb > Cs","Li > K > Na > Rb > Cs","Li = Na = K = Rb = Cs, because each gives one electron to the sea","Li > Na > K > Rb > Cs"],4,"Each gives one electron to the sea, but down the group the cations get larger, so the attraction between cations and the electron sea weakens: Li 181 °C > Na 98 °C > K 64 °C > Rb 39 °C > Cs 28 °C. Same number of electrons does not mean equal bonding strength; size matters.",["The number of valence electrons is the same.","What changes down the group?","Bigger cations → weaker metallic bonding."]],
["m8-a-110",8,16,4,"Concept","mcq","Metals tend to have moderate melting points but very high boiling points (gallium melts at 30 °C but boils at 2403 °C). Why?",["Metallic bonds break completely on melting and reform on boiling","Melting only lets the cations move past each other within the electron sea, but boiling must pull individual atoms out of the sea entirely","Boiling requires ionizing every metal atom","Liquid metals form covalent molecules that must be broken to boil","The electron sea freezes when the metal melts"],1,"On melting, the cations can slide past one another but stay inside the electron sea, so most of the metallic bonding remains (modest energy). Boiling separates atoms completely from the sea and from each other, which takes far more energy. Boiling produces gaseous atoms, not ions; liquid metals are not molecular.",["What is still holding the atoms together in the liquid metal?","Compare 'moving within the sea' with 'leaving the sea'.","Boiling must remove atoms from the electron sea."]],
["m8-a-111",8,16,3,"Exam","mcq","Which explanation best accounts for Mg (mp 649 °C) melting much higher than Na (mp 98 °C)?",["Mg is more electronegative, so its bonds are ionic","Mg forms Mg₂ molecules held by covalent bonds","Each Mg atom gives two valence electrons to the sea and forms a smaller 2+ cation than Na⁺, so the metallic bonding is stronger","Na atoms are smaller than Mg atoms, so they pack less efficiently","Mg has a lower first ionization energy than Na"],2,"Metallic bonding strength rises with more valence electrons per atom and a smaller, more highly charged cation: Mg (2 e⁻, Mg²⁺) beats Na (1 e⁻, larger Na⁺). Mg metal is not ionic or molecular; Na atoms are larger, not smaller, than Mg atoms; and Mg's IE₁ (738) is higher than Na's (496).",["Count valence electrons per atom.","More electrons in the sea + smaller cation → stronger bonding.","Two vs one."]],
["m9-a-001",9,17,1,"Lecture","mcq","Which of these is a correct step in drawing a Lewis structure, done BEFORE any bonds or lone pairs are drawn?",["Count the total number of valence electrons, adjusting for any ionic charge","Place a double bond between every pair of atoms","Put the most electronegative atom in the centre","Assign formal charges to every atom","Give every atom, including H, an octet"],0,"Silberberg's recipe: (1) arrange the atoms - the atom with the lower group number (lower electronegativity, never H) goes in the centre; (2) count the total valence electrons (add one per negative charge, subtract one per positive charge); (3) draw a single bond from each outer atom to the centre (2 electrons each); (4) distribute the rest as lone pairs, outer atoms first, then the centre; (5) if the centre lacks an octet, turn outer lone pairs into multiple bonds. Formal charges are checked last, H only gets 2 electrons, and the most electronegative atom usually goes on the outside.",["You need to know how many electrons you have to place.","Sum the valence electrons of all atoms.","Then adjust for the charge of an ion."]],
["m9-a-002",9,17,2,"Calculation","num","How many valence electrons must be shown in the Lewis structure of the sulfate ion, SO₄²⁻? (Enter a whole number.)",["32"],32,"S: 6; O: 4 × 6 = 24; the 2− charge adds 2 electrons. Total = 6 + 24 + 2 = 32. Forgetting the charge gives 30; subtracting it gives 28.",["S and O are both in group 6A.","Add 2 electrons for the 2− charge.","6 + 4(6) + 2."],{"abs":0}],
["m9-a-003",9,17,2,"Calculation","num","How many valence electrons are in the ammonium ion, NH₄⁺? (Enter a whole number.)",["8"],8,"N: 5; H: 4 × 1 = 4; the 1+ charge REMOVES one electron: 5 + 4 − 1 = 8 (four N−H bonds, no lone pairs). Adding the charge instead gives 10.",["A positive charge means electrons were lost.","5 + 4, then adjust for +1.","Subtract one electron."],{"abs":0}],
["m9-a-004",9,17,2,"Calculation","mcq","How many valence electrons are in the chlorate ion, ClO₃⁻?",["25","24","26","27","32"],2,"Cl: 7; O: 3 × 6 = 18; the 1− charge adds 1. Total 7 + 18 + 1 = 26. 25 ignores the charge; 24 subtracts it; 27 adds 2 for a 1− charge; 32 is the count for ClO₄⁻.",["Cl is in group 7A, O in 6A.","Add one electron for the 1− charge.","7 + 18 + 1."]],
["m9-a-005",9,17,2,"Calculation","num","How many valence electrons are in XeF₄? (Enter a whole number.)",["36"],36,"Xe (noble gas, group 8A): 8; F: 4 × 7 = 28. Total = 36. (Four Xe−F bonds use 8, the F lone pairs use 24, leaving 4 = two lone pairs on Xe.)",["Xe has 8 valence electrons.","Each F has 7.","8 + 4(7)."],{"abs":0}],
["m9-a-006",9,17,2,"Calculation","mcq","How many valence electrons are in the thiocyanate ion, SCN⁻?",["15","16","17","14","18"],1,"S: 6, C: 4, N: 5, plus 1 for the 1− charge: 6 + 4 + 5 + 1 = 16. 15 ignores the charge; 14 subtracts it; 17 and 18 add extra electrons.",["Group numbers: S 6A, C 4A, N 5A.","Then the charge.","6 + 4 + 5 + 1."]],
["m9-a-007",9,17,1,"Concept","mcq","Which atom is the central atom in the Lewis structure of HCN?",["H","N","Either H or N, since both are bonded to C only once","C","None; H, C and N form a triangle"],3,"H can form only one bond, so it is never central. Of C and N, the less electronegative atom (C, 2.5 vs N 3.0) goes in the centre: H−C≡N. The atoms are in a line, not a triangle.",["Which atom can form only one bond?","The central atom is usually the least electronegative (never H).","C is less electronegative than N."]],
["m9-a-008",9,17,1,"Concept","mcq","In a Lewis structure, the central atom is usually:",["the atom with the highest electronegativity","hydrogen, whenever it is present","the atom with the most valence electrons","the heaviest atom, even if it is H","the atom with the lowest electronegativity, but never H"],4,"The central atom shares electrons with several neighbours, so it is normally the least electronegative atom (e.g. S in SO₃, C in CO₂, Cl in ClO₃⁻). H forms only one bond and is always terminal. The most electronegative atoms (F, O) usually sit on the outside.",["Which atoms are most often on the outside: F and O, or C and S?","The central atom shares its electrons with several partners.","Lowest EN, never H."]],
["m9-a-009",9,17,2,"Concept","mcq","Which is the correct Lewis structure of N₂?",["N=N with two lone pairs on each N","N−N with three lone pairs on each N","N≡N with no lone pairs","N=N with one lone pair on each N","N≡N with one lone pair on each N (10 valence electrons)"],4,"N₂ has 2 × 5 = 10 valence electrons. A triple bond (6) plus one lone pair on each N (4) uses all 10 and gives each N an octet. N=N with two lone pairs each needs 12 electrons; N−N with three each needs 14; N≡N alone uses only 6; N=N with one pair each uses 8 and leaves each N with 6.",["Count the valence electrons: 2 × 5.","Each N needs an octet using only one partner.","Three shared pairs plus one lone pair each."]],
["m9-a-010",9,17,2,"Concept","mcq","Which is the correct Lewis structure of CO₂ (16 valence electrons)?",["O−C−O, with three lone pairs on each O and none on C","O=C=O, with two lone pairs on each O and none on C","O=C=O, with two lone pairs on each O and one lone pair on C","O−C−O, with three lone pairs on each O and two lone pairs on C","C=O=O, with two lone pairs on each end atom"],1,"C (least electronegative) is central. Single bonds + O octets use 4 + 12 = 16 electrons, but C has only 4 → move a lone pair from each O into a bond: O=C=O, two lone pairs on each O, C with an octet, 16 electrons. O−C−O leaves C with 4 electrons; adding lone pairs to C needs 18 or 20 electrons; O is not the central atom.",["C goes in the centre.","If the centre lacks an octet, make multiple bonds.","Two double bonds."]],
["m9-a-011",9,17,2,"Concept","mcq","In formaldehyde, H₂CO (C is the central atom), which bond is a double bond?",["One of the C−H bonds","Both C−H bonds","None; all bonds are single and C has a lone pair","The C=O bond","A bond between the two H atoms"],3,"H₂CO has 4 + 2 + 6 = 12 valence electrons. Single bonds C−H, C−H, C−O (6) + three lone pairs on O (6) = 12, but C has only 6 → one O lone pair becomes a second C−O bond: C=O with two lone pairs on O. H can hold only 2 electrons, so it never forms a double bond, and the H atoms are not bonded to each other.",["H can only ever form one single bond.","Count: 12 valence electrons; check C's octet.","Make the multiple bond to O."]],
["m9-a-012",9,17,1,"Concept","mcq","How many electron pairs are shared between the two carbon atoms in ethylene, C₂H₄?",["1","3","2","4","0"],2,"C₂H₄ has 2(4) + 4(1) = 12 valence electrons. Five single bonds (C−C and four C−H) use 10, leaving 2; giving each C an octet requires a C=C double bond: H₂C=CH₂. Two shared pairs between the carbons.",["Count valence electrons: 12.","Each C must end up with an octet.","A double bond."]],
["m9-a-013",9,17,3,"Concept","mcq","How many lone pairs are in the Lewis structure of HCN?",["1 (on N)","0","2 (one on C and one on N)","3 (all on N)","4"],0,"HCN: 1 + 4 + 5 = 10 valence electrons. H−C≡N uses 8 in bonds (one single + one triple), leaving 2 = one lone pair on N. C already has an octet (4 bonding pairs), so it has no lone pair. Three pairs on N would fit a single C−N bond, which leaves C short.",["Draw H−C≡N.","10 electrons total; subtract the bonding electrons.","10 − 8 = 2."]],
["m9-a-014",9,17,2,"Concept","mcq","Which is the correct Lewis structure of carbon monoxide, CO?",["C=O with two lone pairs on O and one lone pair on C","C=O with two lone pairs on each atom","C≡O with one lone pair on C and one lone pair on O","C−O with three lone pairs on O and one lone pair on C","C≡O with no lone pairs"],2,"CO has 4 + 6 = 10 valence electrons. :C≡O: uses 6 bonding + 4 lone-pair electrons and gives both atoms an octet. C=O with 2 + 1 lone pairs uses 10 but leaves C with only 6 electrons; C=O with two pairs each needs 12; C−O with 3 + 1 pairs leaves C with 4; C≡O alone uses only 6.",["10 valence electrons.","Both atoms need octets, sharing as much as possible.","Same pattern as N₂."]],
["m9-a-015",9,17,3,"Calculation","num","How many lone pairs are there in total (on all atoms) in the Lewis structure of CO₂? (Enter a whole number.)",["4"],4,"O=C=O: 16 valence electrons; 8 are in the two double bonds; the remaining 8 = 4 lone pairs, two on each O and none on C.",["Draw O=C=O.","Subtract the bonding electrons from 16.","8 lone-pair electrons."],{"abs":0}],
["m9-a-016",9,17,3,"Calculation","num","How many lone pairs are there in total (on all atoms) in XeF₄? (Enter a whole number.)",["14"],14,"XeF₄: 36 valence electrons. Four Xe−F bonds use 8 → 28 left = 14 lone pairs: three on each F (12) and two on Xe. Counting only Xe's lone pairs gives 2; only F's gives 12.",["Total valence electrons: 8 + 28.","Subtract 8 bonding electrons and divide by 2.","12 on the F atoms + 2 on Xe."],{"abs":0}],
["m9-a-017",9,17,1,"Concept","mcq","What does resonance mean?",["The molecule rapidly flips back and forth between the structures","Two or more valid Lewis structures differ only in where electrons are placed, and the real species is an average (hybrid) of them","The structures differ in how the atoms are connected","Different compounds share the same molecular formula","Every atom in each structure has zero formal charge"],1,"Resonance structures have the same atom positions but different electron (bond and lone-pair) placements. The real molecule does not flip between them: it is one blended structure, e.g. NO₃⁻ has three identical N−O bonds. Different connectivity means isomers, not resonance; resonance forms can carry formal charges.",["Do the atoms move between resonance structures?","Only the electrons are placed differently.","The real thing is a blend, all the time."]],
["m9-a-018",9,17,2,"Concept","mcq","How many equivalent resonance structures can be drawn for the nitrate ion, NO₃⁻ (octets, N central)?",["1","2","4","6","3"],4,"NO₃⁻ (24 electrons) has one N=O and two N−O bonds. The double bond can be placed on any of the three O atoms, giving three equivalent resonance structures.",["How many N−O bonds are double in one structure?","How many places can that double bond go?","Three O atoms."]],
["m9-a-019",9,17,3,"Concept","mcq","What is the N−O bond order in the nitrate ion, NO₃⁻?",["1","2","3/2 (1.5)","4/3 (about 1.33)","3"],3,"Each resonance structure has one double and two single bonds: 4 shared pairs spread over 3 N−O links → average bond order 4/3 ≈ 1.33. 3/2 is the value for NO₂⁻ or O₃ (3 pairs over 2 links).",["Count the total shared pairs between N and the O atoms in one structure.","Divide by the number of N−O bonds.","(2 + 1 + 1)/3."]],
["m9-a-020",9,17,3,"Concept","mcq","Which statement about the C−O bonds in the carbonate ion, CO₃²⁻, is correct?",["All three are identical, each with a bond order of 4/3","One is a double bond (shorter) and two are single bonds (longer)","All three are double bonds","Two have bond order 3/2 and one has bond order 1","They change between single and double many times a second"],0,"CO₃²⁻ (24 electrons) has three resonance structures, each with one C=O and two C−O. The hybrid has three identical C−O bonds, bond order (2 + 1 + 1)/3 = 4/3, all the same length. One drawn structure is not the real ion, and the ion does not flip.",["How many resonance structures are there?","Average the bond orders over the three bonds.","4 shared pairs over 3 bonds."]],
["m9-a-021",9,17,3,"Calculation","num","What is the average O−O bond order in ozone, O₃? (Enter a number.)",["1.5"],1.5,"O₃ (18 electrons) has two resonance structures, O=O−O and O−O=O. Each O−O link is double in one and single in the other: 3 shared pairs over 2 bonds → bond order 1.5. Both O−O bonds are identical (128 pm).",["Draw both resonance structures.","Count the shared pairs and divide by the number of O−O bonds.","(2 + 1)/2."],{"abs":0.01}],
["m9-a-022",9,17,2,"Concept","mcq","Which species has two equivalent resonance structures, giving an average bond order of 1.5 between the central atom and each O?",["NO₃⁻","CO₃²⁻","CO₂","H₂O","NO₂⁻"],4,"NO₂⁻ (18 electrons): O=N−O and O−N=O; 3 shared pairs over 2 N−O bonds → 1.5. NO₃⁻ and CO₃²⁻ have three resonance structures and bond order 4/3; CO₂ has two full double bonds (order 2); H₂O has no resonance.",["Look for one double bond shared between two equivalent positions.","Bond order = shared pairs ÷ number of bonds.","3 pairs over 2 bonds."]],
["m9-a-023",9,17,3,"Concept","mcq","In the formate ion, HCO₂⁻ (C bonded to H and to both O atoms), how do the two C−O bonds compare?",["One is C=O (shorter) and the other is C−O (longer)","They are equal in length, each with a bond order of 1.5","Both are double bonds","Both are single bonds and C carries a lone pair","Both have a bond order of 4/3"],1,"HCO₂⁻ has 1 + 4 + 12 + 1 = 18 valence electrons. Each structure has one C=O and one C−O(⁻), and the double bond can sit on either O → two resonance structures. The hybrid has two identical C−O bonds of order 3/2. 4/3 would need three equivalent O atoms, as in carbonate.",["Draw one structure, then move the double bond.","Two equivalent structures.","3 shared pairs over 2 C−O bonds."]],
["m9-a-024",9,17,3,"Concept","mcq","Which statement about the carbon-carbon bonds in benzene, C₆H₆, is correct?",["All six are identical, with a bond order of 1.5 (between single and double)","Three short C=C bonds alternate with three long C−C bonds","All six are double bonds","All six are single bonds","All six have a bond order of 4/3"],0,"Benzene has two resonance structures with alternating single and double bonds around the ring. Each C−C link is double in one and single in the other, so all six bonds are identical with bond order 1.5 (139 pm, between C−C 154 and C=C 134 pm). The alternating picture is only one resonance form.",["Draw the two alternating-bond structures.","Each ring bond is single in one, double in the other.","Average of 1 and 2."]],
["m9-a-025",9,17,4,"Exam","mcq","Arrange in order of INCREASING carbon-oxygen bond length: CO₃²⁻, CO, CH₃OH, CO₂.",["CH₃OH < CO₃²⁻ < CO₂ < CO","CO < CO₃²⁻ < CO₂ < CH₃OH","CO₂ < CO < CO₃²⁻ < CH₃OH","CO < CO₂ < CO₃²⁻ < CH₃OH","CO < CO₂ < CH₃OH < CO₃²⁻"],3,"Find each C−O bond order: CO is C≡O (3); CO₂ has two C=O (2); CO₃²⁻ resonance gives 4/3; CH₃OH has a C−O single bond (1). Higher bond order → shorter bond, so CO < CO₂ < CO₃²⁻ < CH₃OH. Treating carbonate as having a full double bond puts it before CO₂.",["Draw each Lewis structure and find the C−O bond order.","Remember carbonate's bond order is a resonance average.","Orders 3, 2, 4/3, 1."]],
["m9-a-026",9,17,4,"Exam","mcq","Arrange in order of DECREASING nitrogen-oxygen bond length: NO⁺, NO₃⁻, NO₂⁻, NO₂⁺ (O=N=O).",["NO⁺ > NO₂⁺ > NO₂⁻ > NO₃⁻","NO₂⁻ > NO₃⁻ > NO₂⁺ > NO⁺","NO₃⁻ > NO₂⁻ > NO₂⁺ > NO⁺","NO₃⁻ > NO₂⁺ > NO₂⁻ > NO⁺","NO₃⁻ > NO₂⁻ > NO⁺ > NO₂⁺"],2,"Bond orders: NO⁺ (10 e⁻, like N₂) N≡O = 3; NO₂⁺ (16 e⁻, like CO₂) two N=O = 2; NO₂⁻ resonance 3/2; NO₃⁻ resonance 4/3. Longest bond = lowest order: NO₃⁻ (1.33) > NO₂⁻ (1.5) > NO₂⁺ (2) > NO⁺ (3).",["Count valence electrons for each species to get its Lewis structure.","Resonance averages: 4/3 for NO₃⁻, 3/2 for NO₂⁻.","Lowest bond order = longest bond."]],
["m9-a-027",9,17,1,"Lecture","mcq","How is the formal charge (FC) of an atom in a Lewis structure calculated?",["FC = valence electrons − (lone-pair electrons + all bonding electrons)","FC = valence electrons − (lone-pair electrons + ½ bonding electrons)","FC = valence electrons − number of bonds","FC = (lone-pair electrons + ½ bonding electrons) − valence electrons","FC = number of bonds − number of lone pairs"],1,"Formal charge assumes bonding electrons are shared equally: the atom 'owns' all its lone-pair electrons and half of each bonding pair. FC = V − (L + ½B). Taking all bonding electrons is the oxidation-number idea gone wrong; reversing the subtraction flips the sign.",["The atom owns its lone pairs fully.","Bonding electrons are split equally between the two atoms.","V − (L + ½B)."]],
["m9-a-028",9,17,2,"Calculation","mcq","What is the formal charge on N in the ammonium ion, NH₄⁺?",["0","−1","+4","−3","+1"],4,"N has 5 valence electrons; in NH₄⁺ it has 0 lone-pair electrons and 4 bonds (8 bonding electrons). FC = 5 − (0 + ½ × 8) = 5 − 4 = +1. Each H: 1 − (0 + 1) = 0. Sum = +1, the ion's charge. −3 is nitrogen's oxidation number, not its formal charge.",["N in NH₄⁺ has four bonds and no lone pairs.","FC = 5 − (0 + 4).","Formal charges must add to +1."]],
["m9-a-029",9,17,2,"Calculation","num","What is the formal charge on O in the hydronium ion, H₃O⁺ (three O−H bonds, one lone pair on O)? (Enter a number with its sign.)",["+1"],1,"O: 6 valence electrons; 2 lone-pair electrons; 3 bonds = 6 bonding electrons. FC = 6 − (2 + 3) = +1. Each H is 0, so the formal charges add to +1, the charge of the ion.",["FC = V − (L + ½B).","L = 2, B = 6 for this O.","6 − (2 + 3)."],{"abs":0}],
["m9-a-030",9,17,3,"Calculation","mcq","For the ozone structure O=O−O (left O double-bonded with two lone pairs, central O with one lone pair, right O single-bonded with three lone pairs), what are the formal charges (left, centre, right)?",["0, 0, 0","0, −1, +1","+1, 0, −1","0, +1, −1","0, +2, −2"],3,"Left O: 6 − (4 + 2) = 0. Centre O: 6 − (2 + 3) = +1. Right O: 6 − (6 + 1) = −1. Sum = 0 for neutral O₃. Writing 0, 0, 0 forgets that the central O has three bonds and a lone pair.",["Use FC = V − (L + ½B) for each O.","The central O has 3 bonds and 1 lone pair.","Centre: 6 − (2 + 3)."]],
["m9-a-031",9,17,3,"Calculation","num","In the Lewis structure of SO₄²⁻ drawn with four S−O single bonds (S has an octet, each O has three lone pairs), what is the formal charge on S? (Enter a number with its sign.)",["+2"],2,"S: 6 valence electrons, 0 lone-pair electrons, 4 bonds (8 bonding electrons): FC = 6 − (0 + 4) = +2. Each O: 6 − (6 + 1) = −1. Check: +2 + 4(−1) = −2, the ion's charge.",["S has four bonds and no lone pairs here.","FC = 6 − (0 + ½ × 8).","Check the sum equals −2."],{"abs":0}],
["m9-a-032",9,17,4,"Exam","mcq","For SO₄²⁻, compare the structure with four S−O single bonds (S has an octet) with the structure with two S=O and two S−O bonds (12 electrons around S). Based on formal charges alone, which structure is preferred, and why?",["The all-single structure, because S can never exceed an octet","The all-single structure, because its formal charges add to −2 and the other's do not","The one with two S=O bonds: S 0, the two double-bonded O 0, the two single-bonded O −1, closer to zero than S +2 and four O −1","The two-double structure, because every atom in it has zero formal charge","Neither, because both have the same number of electrons"],2,"All-single: S = 6 − 4 = +2; each O = −1. Two S=O: S = 6 − (0 + 6) = 0; each S=O oxygen = 6 − (4 + 2) = 0; each S−O oxygen = −1. Both sum to −2, but the second has smaller formal charges, so the formal-charge rule prefers it (S, in period 3, can expand its octet). Two O atoms still carry −1, so not every FC is zero. (Whether S really holds 12 electrons is debated - modern calculations favour the octet structure with very polar S−O bonds - which is why the question says 'based on formal charges alone'; by that rule, the textbook's, the two-double-bond structure wins.)",["Calculate the formal charge of S in each structure.","Both sets add up to −2; which set is closer to zero?","S: +2 vs 0."]],
["m9-a-033",9,17,4,"Trap","mcq","For carbon monoxide, :C≡O:, what are the formal charges of C and O, and how do they compare with the oxidation numbers?",["Formal charges C −1, O +1; oxidation numbers C +2, O −2","Formal charges C +2, O −2; oxidation numbers C −1, O +1","Formal charges C 0, O 0; oxidation numbers C +2, O −2","Formal charges and oxidation numbers are both C +2, O −2","Formal charges C +1, O −1; oxidation numbers C +2, O −2"],0,"Formal charge (bonding electrons shared equally): C = 4 − (2 + 3) = −1; O = 6 − (2 + 3) = +1. Oxidation number (all bonding electrons given to the more electronegative O): O 'owns' 8 → 6 − 8 = −2; C owns 2 → 4 − 2 = +2. The two bookkeeping methods differ, and here the formal charges even put the negative charge on the less electronegative atom.",["Formal charge splits bonding electrons equally.","Oxidation numbers give both electrons of each bond to the more electronegative atom.","C: 4 − (2 + 3); O as an oxide: −2."]],
["m9-a-034",9,17,4,"Exam","mcq","Three resonance structures with octets can be drawn for the cyanate ion, OCN⁻ (C central): [O=C=N]⁻, [O≡C−N]⁻ and [O−C≡N]⁻. Judged by the formal-charge rules, which contributes MOST to the real ion?",["[O=C=N]⁻, with formal charges O 0, C 0, N −1","[O≡C−N]⁻, with formal charges O +1, C 0, N −2","[O−C≡N]⁻, with formal charges O −1, C 0, N 0","[O=C=N]⁻, because two double bonds are always better than one triple bond","All three contribute equally"],2,"Formal charges: O−C≡N: O 6 − (6 + 1) = −1, C 0, N 5 − (2 + 3) = 0. O=C=N: O 0, C 0, N 5 − (4 + 2) = −1. O≡C−N: O +1, N −2 (worst). The first two both have small charges, but the rule puts the negative charge on the more electronegative atom, O (3.5 vs N 3.0), so [O−C≡N]⁻ is best. The structures are not equivalent, so they do not contribute equally.",["Compute formal charges in each structure.","Smallest charges first; then negative charge on the more electronegative atom.","O is more electronegative than N."]],
["m9-a-035",9,17,4,"Exam","mcq","N₂O has the skeleton N−N−O. According to the formal-charge rules (charges closest to zero; any negative formal charge on the more electronegative atom), which resonance structure is MOST important?",["N≡N−O, with formal charges 0, +1, −1","N=N=O, with formal charges −1, +1, 0","N−N≡O, with formal charges −2, +1, +1","N=N=O, with formal charges 0, 0, 0","N≡N−O, with formal charges 0, 0, 0"],0,"16 valence electrons. N≡N−O (lone pairs 1, 0, 3): terminal N 5 − (2 + 3) = 0; central N 5 − (0 + 4) = +1; O 6 − (6 + 1) = −1. N=N=O (2, 0, 2): −1, +1, 0. N−N≡O (3, 0, 1): −2, +1, +1. The first two have equally small charges, but N≡N−O places the −1 on O, the more electronegative atom, so it is the most important. The central N always has four bonds and no lone pair, so its formal charge is +1, never 0. (The measured bond lengths show N=N=O also contributes substantially; the formal-charge rules rank N≡N−O first, and that ranking is what is asked.)",["Compute formal charges for each structure.","Smallest charges, then negative on the most electronegative atom.","The −1 belongs on O."]],
["m9-a-036",9,17,4,"Exam","mcq","Apply the formal-charge rules (formal charges as close to zero as possible; any negative formal charge on the more electronegative atom; EN: S 2.5, N 3.0) to the thiocyanate ion, SCN⁻ (C central, octets on all atoms). Which resonance structure do these rules rank as most important?",["[S−C≡N]⁻, with formal charges S −1, C 0, N 0","[S=C=N]⁻, with formal charges S 0, C 0, N −1","[S≡C−N]⁻, with formal charges S +1, C 0, N −2","[S=C=N]⁻, with formal charges S −1, C 0, N 0","[S−C≡N]⁻, with formal charges S 0, C 0, N −1"],1,"16 electrons. S=C=N (2 lone pairs on S and N): S 6 − (4 + 2) = 0, C 0, N 5 − (4 + 2) = −1. S−C≡N (3 on S, 1 on N): S 6 − (6 + 1) = −1, C 0, N 0. S≡C−N: S +1, N −2. The first two both have one −1; the rule puts it on the more electronegative atom, N, so [S=C=N]⁻ is preferred. The last two choices have the formal charges calculated wrongly. (Real SCN⁻ also has a large [S−C≡N]⁻ contribution - its measured C−N bond is nearly a triple bond - so this is a question about what the formal-charge rules predict, which is how Silberberg-style problems ask it.)",["Find the two structures with only one nonzero formal charge.","Which atom should hold the −1?","N (3.0) is more electronegative than S (2.5)."]],
["m9-a-037",9,17,3,"Concept","mcq","The atoms in N₂O could be arranged N−N−O or N−O−N. Which arrangement is better, and why?",["N−O−N, because the most electronegative atom always goes in the centre","N−O−N, because it is symmetric","Both are equally good, because both have 16 valence electrons","N−N−O, because N−O−N would put a large positive formal charge (+2) on the most electronegative atom, O","N−N−O, because an N−N bond is stronger than an N−O bond"],3,"For N=O=N, the central O has four bonds and no lone pair: FC = 6 − (0 + 4) = +2, and each N is −1. A +2 charge on the most electronegative atom is very unfavourable. N−N−O allows structures with charges of only 0, +1, −1. The central atom is usually the LEAST electronegative; symmetry and electron count do not decide it.",["Try formal charges for the O-centred skeleton.","Central O with four bonds and no lone pairs.","FC(O) = 6 − 4."]],
["m9-a-038",9,17,2,"Concept","mcq","Which statement about formal charges and choosing the most important resonance structure is FALSE?",["Smaller formal charges (closer to zero) are preferable","A negative formal charge should be on the more electronegative atom","Formal charges must add up to the overall charge of the species","Like charges on adjacent atoms are not desirable","Positive formal charges should be placed on the most electronegative atom"],4,"The rules: formal charges closest to zero; negative formal charges on the more electronegative atoms; same-sign charges on neighbouring atoms are unfavourable; and the formal charges always add up to the species' charge. Positive charge on the most electronegative atom is the OPPOSITE of what is preferred.",["Which way should a negative charge go?","More electronegative atoms prefer extra electron density.","Positive charge on the most EN atom is bad."]],
["m9-a-039",9,17,3,"Calculation","mcq","In the chlorate ion, ClO₃⁻, drawn with three Cl−O single bonds and octets on every atom, what is the formal charge on Cl?",["+1","0","−1","+3","+2"],4,"26 electrons: three Cl−O bonds (6) + three lone pairs on each O (18) + one lone pair on Cl (2). FC(Cl) = 7 − (2 + 3) = +2; each O = 6 − (6 + 1) = −1. Sum: +2 − 3 = −1 ✓. Giving Cl no lone pair gives +3 (and uses only 24 electrons); −1 confuses the ion's overall charge with the Cl atom's formal charge.",["Count 26 valence electrons and place them.","Cl ends with three bonds and one lone pair.","7 − (2 + 3)."]],
["m9-a-040",9,17,4,"Exam","mcq","Which statement about the nitrate ion, NO₃⁻, is FALSE?",["It has 24 valence electrons","The formal charge on N is +1 in each resonance structure","Each N−O bond has a bond order of 4/3","One N−O bond is shorter than the other two","It has three equivalent resonance structures"],3,"24 electrons (5 + 18 + 1); in every resonance structure N has four bonds and no lone pair, FC = 5 − 4 = +1; three equivalent structures; bond order 4/3. Because of resonance all three N−O bonds are the SAME length, so 'one bond is shorter' is false - it describes a single resonance structure, not the real ion.",["Check each statement against the resonance hybrid.","Does the real ion have a distinct double bond?","All three bonds are equal."]],
["m9-a-041",9,17,1,"Concept","mcq","How many valence electrons surround boron in BF₃?",["8","6","4","10","3"],1,"BF₃ has 3 + 21 = 24 electrons: three B−F single bonds (6) and three lone pairs on each F (18). B has only 6 electrons around it - an electron-deficient atom. (A B=F double bond would put a + formal charge on F, the most electronegative atom.)",["Draw three B−F single bonds.","Boron has no lone pairs left.","3 bonds × 2 electrons."]],
["m9-a-042",9,17,2,"Concept","mcq","Which species has an electron-deficient central atom with only FOUR valence electrons around it?",["BF₃","CO₂","BeCl₂","NO","H₂O"],2,"BeCl₂: 2 + 14 = 16 electrons; two Be−Cl bonds and three lone pairs on each Cl, so Be has only 4 electrons. In BF₃, B has 6; C in CO₂ and O in H₂O have octets; NO is an odd-electron molecule.",["Be has only 2 valence electrons.","Each Be−Cl bond gives Be 2 electrons.","2 bonds = 4 electrons."]],
["m9-a-043",9,17,2,"Concept","mcq","Which molecule has an odd number of valence electrons (a free radical)?",["NO₂","CO₂","SO₂","O₃","NO₂⁻"],0,"NO₂: 5 + 2(6) = 17 electrons, odd, so one atom must have an unpaired electron. CO₂ 16, SO₂ 18, O₃ 18 and NO₂⁻ 18 are all even.",["Add up the valence electrons for each.","N contributes an odd number (5).","5 + 12 = 17."]],
["m9-a-044",9,17,3,"Concept","mcq","Which pair are BOTH odd-electron molecules?",["NO and ClO₂","NO and CO","NO₂ and NO₂⁻","ClO₂ and SO₂","NO⁺ and CO"],0,"NO: 5 + 6 = 11 (odd). ClO₂: 7 + 12 = 19 (odd). CO 10, NO₂⁻ 18, SO₂ 18 and NO⁺ 10 are even, so each wrong pair contains at least one even-electron species.",["Count valence electrons for each species.","N and Cl each contribute an odd number.","11 and 19."]],
["m9-a-045",9,17,3,"Concept","mcq","Why can phosphorus form PCl₅ while nitrogen cannot form NCl₅?",["N is more electronegative than P","N atoms are larger than P atoms","P has more valence electrons than N","P is in period 3 and can use empty d orbitals to hold more than eight electrons; N, in period 2, has no d orbitals in its valence shell","NCl₅ would have an odd number of electrons"],3,"An expanded valence shell (10 or 12 electrons) is possible only for a central atom in period 3 or below, which has empty d orbitals available (and is larger). N is in period 2 (only 2s and 2p), so it is limited to an octet. P and N both have 5 valence electrons; N is smaller, not larger; NCl₅ would have 40 electrons (even).",["Which period is each element in?","Expanded octets need a period-3-or-lower central atom.","Period 2 atoms have no d orbitals."]],
["m9-a-046",9,17,2,"Concept","mcq","How many electrons surround the central S atom in SF₆?",["8","12","6","10","14"],1,"Six S−F single bonds × 2 electrons = 12 electrons around S: an expanded octet (possible for period 3 S). S has no lone pair (48 electrons: 12 in bonds + 36 in F lone pairs).",["Count the S−F bonds.","Each bond puts 2 electrons around S.","Six bonds × 2 electrons each."]],
["m9-a-047",9,17,2,"Concept","mcq","In which species does the central atom have an expanded octet (more than 8 electrons)?",["N in NH₄⁺","B in BF₃","C in CO₃²⁻","O in H₂O","I in I₃⁻"],4,"I₃⁻ (22 electrons): the central I has 2 bonds and 3 lone pairs = 10 electrons. N in NH₄⁺ and C in CO₃²⁻ and O in H₂O have octets; B in BF₃ has only 6.",["Expanded octets need a central atom from period 3 or below.","Draw I₃⁻ and count around the middle I.","2 bonds + 3 lone pairs."]],
["m9-a-048",9,17,3,"Calculation","num","In XeF₂ (22 valence electrons), how many lone pairs are on the central Xe atom? (Enter a whole number.)",["3"],3,"Two Xe−F bonds use 4 electrons; three lone pairs on each F use 12; 22 − 16 = 6 electrons = 3 lone pairs on Xe (10 electrons around Xe). This makes XeF₂ AX₂E₃.",["Place bonds and the F lone pairs first.","22 − 4 − 12 = ?","6 electrons left on Xe."],{"abs":0}],
["m9-a-049",9,17,4,"Trap","mcq","A student draws ClF₃ with three Cl−F single bonds, three lone pairs on each F, and one lone pair on Cl, then stops because Cl has an octet. What is wrong?",["Nothing; Cl can never have more than an octet","Cl should form a double bond to one F atom","Only 26 of the 28 valence electrons have been placed; the last two go on Cl as a second lone pair, giving Cl 10 electrons","Each F should have only two lone pairs","Cl should have three lone pairs"],2,"ClF₃ has 7 + 21 = 28 valence electrons. Bonds (6) + F lone pairs (18) + one Cl lone pair (2) = 26, so 2 electrons are still unplaced; they go on the central Cl, which (period 3) can expand: 3 bonds + 2 lone pairs = 10 electrons. That is AX₃E₂ → T-shaped. A Cl=F bond would put a + formal charge on F; F needs three lone pairs; three lone pairs on Cl would need 30 electrons.",["Count the total valence electrons first.","Have all 28 been used?","Leftover electrons go on the central atom."]],
["m9-a-050",9,17,2,"Concept","mcq","How many σ (sigma) and π (pi) bonds are in HCN?",["3 σ and 1 π","2 σ and 1 π","1 σ and 3 π","4 σ and 0 π","2 σ and 2 π"],4,"H−C≡N: every single bond is one σ bond; a triple bond is one σ + two π. So H−C (1 σ) + C≡N (1 σ + 2 π) = 2 σ and 2 π. Counting the triple bond as three σ (or as 3 π) is the usual error.",["Draw H−C≡N.","Single = 1σ; double = 1σ + 1π; triple = 1σ + 2π.","One single bond and one triple bond."]],
["m9-a-051",9,17,3,"Calculation","num","How many σ (sigma) bonds are in ethylene, C₂H₄? (Enter a whole number.)",["5"],5,"H₂C=CH₂ has four C−H single bonds (4 σ) and one C=C double bond (1 σ + 1 π). Total σ = 5 (and 1 π). Counting the double bond as 2 σ gives 6.",["Draw H₂C=CH₂.","Every bond contains exactly one σ bond.","4 C−H + 1 from C=C."],{"abs":0}],
["m9-a-052",9,18,1,"Lecture","mcq","In VSEPR theory, what counts as ONE electron group around a central atom?",["Only single bonds; a double bond counts as two groups","Each lone pair, and each bond whether single, double or triple","Only bonds; lone pairs are ignored","Only lone pairs","Each individual electron"],1,"VSEPR counts regions of electron density: a lone pair is one group, and a single, double or triple bond to one atom is also just one group (it points in one direction). So CO₂ has 2 groups around C, and SO₂ has 3 around S (two bonds + one lone pair).",["How many directions does a double bond point in?","Lone pairs take up space too.","Multiple bonds count once."]],
["m9-a-053",9,18,1,"Concept","mcq","Four electron groups around a central atom adopt which arrangement, with what ideal angle?",["Tetrahedral, 109.5°","Square planar, 90°","Trigonal planar, 120°","Trigonal pyramidal, 107°","Octahedral, 90°"],0,"Four groups get as far apart as possible by pointing to the corners of a tetrahedron: 109.5°. Square planar (90°) keeps them closer; trigonal pyramidal is a MOLECULAR shape (4 groups, 1 lone pair), not an electron-group arrangement.",["Spread four balloons tied together.","The arrangement is 3-D, not flat.","Corners of a tetrahedron."]],
["m9-a-054",9,18,1,"Concept","mcq","Five electron groups around a central atom adopt which arrangement?",["Octahedral, with 90° angles","Square pyramidal, with 90° angles","Trigonal bipyramidal, with 90° and 120° angles","Pentagonal planar, with 72° angles","Tetrahedral, with 109.5° angles"],2,"Five groups: three in a triangle around the 'equator' (120° apart) and two on the axis above and below (90° to the equator): trigonal bipyramidal. Octahedral is six groups; square pyramidal is a molecular shape (AX₅E); a flat pentagon would crowd the groups.",["Three groups around the middle plus two on the axis.","Equatorial 120°, axial-equatorial 90°.","Trigonal bipyramid."]],
["m9-a-055",9,18,2,"Concept","mcq","What is the molecular shape of CH₄?",["Square planar","Trigonal pyramidal","Trigonal planar","Tetrahedral","Seesaw"],3,"C has four bonding pairs and no lone pairs (AX₄): electron-group arrangement and shape are both tetrahedral, H−C−H = 109.5°. A flat square drawing on paper is misleading; the molecule is three-dimensional.",["Count electron groups on C.","Four bonding groups, no lone pairs.","Its VSEPR class is AX₄."]],
["m9-a-056",9,18,2,"Concept","mcq","What are the electron-group arrangement and the molecular shape of NH₃?",["Trigonal planar; trigonal planar","Tetrahedral; trigonal pyramidal","Tetrahedral; tetrahedral","Trigonal pyramidal; tetrahedral","Trigonal planar; trigonal pyramidal"],1,"N has three bonding pairs and one lone pair (AX₃E): 4 groups → tetrahedral arrangement. The shape names only the atoms: N at the apex of a pyramid with three H's → trigonal pyramidal. Trigonal planar would need 3 groups and no lone pair (like BF₃).",["Count bonds and lone pairs on N.","4 groups → tetrahedral arrangement; one is a lone pair.","Its VSEPR class is AX₃E."]],
["m9-a-057",9,18,2,"Concept","mcq","What are the electron-group arrangement and the molecular shape of H₂O?",["Linear; linear","Trigonal planar; bent","Tetrahedral; tetrahedral","Tetrahedral; trigonal pyramidal","Tetrahedral; bent"],4,"O has two bonds and two lone pairs (AX₂E₂): 4 groups → tetrahedral arrangement; with only two atoms attached the shape is bent (V-shaped), H−O−H ≈ 104.5°. Ignoring the lone pairs gives 'linear', the classic error.",["Don't forget oxygen's two lone pairs.","4 groups, 2 of them lone pairs.","Its VSEPR class is AX₂E₂."]],
["m9-a-058",9,18,2,"Concept","mcq","What is the molecular shape of CO₂?",["Bent","Trigonal planar","Tetrahedral","Linear","T-shaped"],3,"O=C=O: C has two electron groups (each double bond counts as one group) and no lone pairs: AX₂, linear, 180°. Counting each double bond as two groups would wrongly give four groups.",["How many electron groups are on C?","A double bond is one group.","Its VSEPR class is AX₂."]],
["m9-a-059",9,18,2,"Concept","mcq","What is the molecular shape of BF₃?",["Trigonal planar","Trigonal pyramidal","T-shaped","Tetrahedral","Bent"],0,"B has three bonding groups and no lone pair (only 6 electrons around B): AX₃, trigonal planar, 120°. Trigonal pyramidal would need a lone pair on B (like NH₃ or NF₃).",["Does B have a lone pair in BF₃?","Three groups, no lone pairs.","Its VSEPR class is AX₃."]],
["m9-a-060",9,18,3,"Concept","mcq","What are the electron-group arrangement and the molecular shape of SO₂?",["Linear; linear","Tetrahedral; bent","Trigonal planar; bent","Trigonal planar; trigonal planar","Tetrahedral; trigonal pyramidal"],2,"SO₂ (18 electrons) has S bonded to two O atoms with one lone pair on S: 3 electron groups (AX₂E) → trigonal planar arrangement, bent shape, O−S−O ≈ 119°. Unlike CO₂, the central atom keeps a lone pair, so it is not linear; it has 3 groups, not 4.",["Draw SO₂ and look for a lone pair on S.","Two bonds + one lone pair = 3 groups.","Its VSEPR class is AX₂E."]],
["m9-a-061",9,18,3,"Concept","mcq","What is the molecular shape of SO₃?",["Trigonal pyramidal","T-shaped","Tetrahedral","Bent","Trigonal planar"],4,"SO₃ (24 electrons): S bonded to three O atoms and no lone pair on S, so AX₃ → trigonal planar, 120°. It is often confused with sulfite, SO₃²⁻ (26 electrons), which has a lone pair on S and is trigonal pyramidal.",["Count the valence electrons: 6 + 18.","Is any electron pair left on S?","Its VSEPR class is AX₃."]],
["m9-a-062",9,18,2,"Concept","mcq","What is the shape of the nitrate ion, NO₃⁻?",["Trigonal planar","Trigonal pyramidal","Tetrahedral","T-shaped","Bent"],0,"N is bonded to three O atoms with no lone pair (24 electrons; resonance makes all three bonds equal): AX₃, trigonal planar, 120°. The negative charge does not add a lone pair to N.",["Draw one resonance structure.","Count groups on N: three bonds, no lone pair.","Its VSEPR class is AX₃."]],
["m9-a-063",9,18,3,"Concept","mcq","What is the molecular shape of the hydronium ion, H₃O⁺?",["Trigonal planar","Tetrahedral","Trigonal pyramidal","Bent","T-shaped"],2,"H₃O⁺ (8 electrons): three O−H bonds and one lone pair on O. Four groups (tetrahedral arrangement), AX₃E → trigonal pyramidal, like NH₃ (they are isoelectronic).",["Count electrons: 6 + 3 − 1.","Three bonds plus one lone pair on O.","Same as NH₃."]],
["m9-a-064",9,18,2,"Concept","mcq","What is the shape of the ammonium ion, NH₄⁺?",["Trigonal pyramidal","Square planar","Seesaw","Tetrahedral","Trigonal planar"],3,"NH₄⁺ (8 electrons) has four N−H bonds and no lone pair: AX₄, tetrahedral, 109.5°. NH₃'s lone pair became the fourth N−H bond, so the pyramid becomes a tetrahedron.",["Count electrons: 5 + 4 − 1.","Four bonds, no lone pairs.","Its VSEPR class is AX₄."]],
["m9-a-065",9,18,3,"Trap","mcq","What is the molecular shape of the chlorate ion, ClO₃⁻?",["Trigonal planar","Trigonal pyramidal","T-shaped","Tetrahedral","Bent"],1,"ClO₃⁻ has 26 valence electrons. Three Cl−O bonds and the O lone pairs use 24; the remaining 2 are a lone pair on Cl. Four groups (AX₃E) → trigonal pyramidal. 'Trigonal planar' (like NO₃⁻) ignores the lone pair; drawing Cl=O double bonds would not change the count of groups.",["Count valence electrons: 7 + 18 + 1.","After the bonds and O lone pairs, is anything left for Cl?","Three bonds + one lone pair."]],
["m9-a-066",9,18,3,"Concept","mcq","What is the shape of the perchlorate ion, ClO₄⁻?",["Tetrahedral","Square planar","Trigonal pyramidal","Seesaw","Square pyramidal"],0,"ClO₄⁻ (32 electrons): four Cl−O bonds and no lone pair on Cl (whether or not Cl=O double bonds are drawn). AX₄ → tetrahedral, like SO₄²⁻ and PO₄³⁻.",["Count valence electrons: 7 + 24 + 1.","Is there a lone pair on Cl?","Four groups, all bonding."]],
["m9-a-067",9,18,3,"Exam","mcq","What is the molecular shape of PCl₃?",["Trigonal planar","Trigonal pyramidal","T-shaped","Tetrahedral","Trigonal bipyramidal"],1,"PCl₃ has 5 + 21 = 26 valence electrons: three P−Cl bonds, three lone pairs on each Cl (18), and one lone pair on P. AX₃E → trigonal pyramidal, like NH₃. The trap is treating it like BF₃ (no lone pair) or PCl₅.",["Count valence electrons: 26.","P keeps one lone pair.","Its VSEPR class is AX₃E."]],
["m9-a-068",9,18,2,"Concept","mcq","What is the molecular shape of PCl₅?",["Square pyramidal","Octahedral","Seesaw","Trigonal bipyramidal","Pentagonal planar"],3,"P has five bonds and no lone pairs (40 electrons: 10 in bonds, 30 on the Cl atoms): AX₅ → trigonal bipyramidal, with 90° and 120° angles.",["Count groups on P.","Five bonds, no lone pairs.","Its VSEPR class is AX₅."]],
["m9-a-069",9,18,4,"Trap","mcq","What is the molecular shape of SF₄?",["Tetrahedral","Square planar","Seesaw","Trigonal pyramidal","T-shaped"],2,"SF₄: 6 + 28 = 34 electrons. Four S−F bonds (8) + F lone pairs (24) = 32, leaving 2 = one lone pair on S. Five groups (trigonal bipyramidal arrangement), AX₄E. The lone pair takes an equatorial position, leaving a seesaw shape. 'Tetrahedral' ignores the lone pair; T-shaped is AX₃E₂.",["Count valence electrons and look for a lone pair on S.","4 bonds + 1 lone pair = 5 groups.","AX₄E, lone pair equatorial."]],
["m9-a-070",9,18,4,"Exam","mcq","What is the molecular shape of ClF₃?",["Trigonal planar","Trigonal pyramidal","Seesaw","Bent","T-shaped"],4,"ClF₃: 28 electrons → three Cl−F bonds and two lone pairs on Cl. Five groups (trigonal bipyramidal), AX₃E₂. Both lone pairs go equatorial, so the three F atoms form a T. Trigonal planar ignores the lone pairs; trigonal pyramidal is AX₃E.",["Count valence electrons: 7 + 21.","3 bonds + 2 lone pairs = 5 groups.","Its VSEPR class is AX₃E₂."]],
["m9-a-071",9,18,4,"Trap","mcq","What is the molecular shape of XeF₂?",["Linear","Bent","T-shaped","Trigonal planar","Seesaw"],0,"XeF₂: 22 electrons → two Xe−F bonds and three lone pairs on Xe. Five groups (trigonal bipyramidal arrangement), AX₂E₃. The three lone pairs occupy the three equatorial positions; the two F atoms are axial, 180° apart → linear. 'Bent' wrongly assumes lone pairs always bend the molecule.",["Count lone pairs on Xe.","Five groups; where do three lone pairs go in a trigonal bipyramid?","Lone pairs equatorial, F atoms axial."]],
["m9-a-072",9,18,4,"Exam","mcq","What is the molecular shape of XeF₄?",["Tetrahedral","Square planar","Seesaw","Square pyramidal","Octahedral"],1,"XeF₄: 36 electrons → four Xe−F bonds and two lone pairs on Xe. Six groups (octahedral arrangement), AX₄E₂. The two lone pairs sit opposite each other (180° apart), leaving the four F atoms in a square plane. 'Tetrahedral' ignores the lone pairs; square pyramidal is AX₅E.",["Count groups: 4 bonds + lone pairs on Xe.","Six groups; two are lone pairs placed opposite each other.","Its VSEPR class is AX₄E₂."]],
["m9-a-073",9,18,2,"Concept","mcq","What is the molecular shape of SF₆?",["Hexagonal planar","Trigonal bipyramidal","Square planar","Octahedral","Trigonal prismatic"],3,"Six S−F bonds and no lone pair on S: AX₆ → octahedral, all adjacent F−S−F angles 90°.",["Count groups on S.","Six bonding groups, no lone pairs.","Its VSEPR class is AX₆."]],
["m9-a-074",9,18,3,"Exam","mcq","What is the molecular shape of BrF₅?",["Trigonal bipyramidal","Octahedral","Square planar","Seesaw","Square pyramidal"],4,"BrF₅: 7 + 35 = 42 electrons → five Br−F bonds (10) + F lone pairs (30) = 40, leaving one lone pair on Br. Six groups (octahedral arrangement), AX₅E → square pyramidal. Trigonal bipyramidal (AX₅) ignores the lone pair.",["Count valence electrons: 42.","5 bonds + 1 lone pair = 6 groups.","Its VSEPR class is AX₅E."]],
["m9-a-075",9,18,4,"Trap","mcq","What is the shape of the triiodide ion, I₃⁻?",["Bent","Trigonal planar","Linear","T-shaped","Trigonal pyramidal"],2,"I₃⁻: 3(7) + 1 = 22 electrons. The central I has two bonds and three lone pairs: AX₂E₃, five groups. The three lone pairs take the equatorial positions, the two outer I atoms are axial → linear (like XeF₂). 'Bent' is the guess if you assume a lone-pair-bearing centre bends.",["Count the electrons, including the 1− charge.","Central I: 2 bonds + 3 lone pairs.","Its VSEPR class is AX₂E₃."]],
["m9-a-076",9,18,4,"Exam","mcq","What is the shape of the ICl₄⁻ ion?",["Tetrahedral","Seesaw","Square pyramidal","Trigonal bipyramidal","Square planar"],4,"ICl₄⁻: 7 + 28 + 1 = 36 electrons. Four I−Cl bonds (8) + Cl lone pairs (24) = 32; 4 left = two lone pairs on I. Six groups, AX₄E₂ → square planar (lone pairs opposite), like XeF₄. Seesaw is AX₄E (one lone pair).",["Count electrons, including the 1− charge.","4 bonds + 2 lone pairs = 6 groups.","Same as XeF₄."]],
["m9-a-077",9,18,3,"Concept","mcq","What is the molecular shape of ozone, O₃?",["Bent","Linear","Trigonal planar","Triangular ring","Trigonal pyramidal"],0,"O₃ (18 electrons): the central O has one double bond, one single bond and one lone pair → three groups (trigonal planar arrangement), AX₂E → bent, angle ≈ 117°. It is not a ring, and not linear because the central atom has a lone pair.",["Draw one resonance structure of O₃.","Look at the lone pairs on the central O.","Like SO₂: AX₂E."]],
["m9-a-078",9,18,2,"Concept","mcq","What is the molecular shape of HCN?",["Bent","Linear","Trigonal planar","Trigonal pyramidal","Tetrahedral"],1,"H−C≡N: C has two electron groups (a single bond and a triple bond) and no lone pair: AX₂ → linear, 180°.",["Count groups on C - the triple bond is one group.","Two groups, no lone pairs.","Its VSEPR class is AX₂."]],
["m9-a-079",9,18,2,"Concept","mcq","What is the molecular shape of formaldehyde, H₂CO (C central)?",["Trigonal pyramidal","Tetrahedral","Trigonal planar","Bent","T-shaped"],2,"C has two C−H bonds and one C=O bond and no lone pair: three groups, AX₃ → trigonal planar. Counting the double bond as two groups would wrongly give a tetrahedral arrangement.",["How many electron groups are on C?","The C=O double bond is one group.","Its VSEPR class is AX₃."]],
["m9-a-080",9,18,3,"Concept","mcq","In acetylene, H−C≡C−H, what is the geometry around each carbon atom?",["Trigonal planar, 120°","Tetrahedral, 109.5°","Bent, about 105°","Linear, 180°","Trigonal pyramidal, 107°"],3,"Each C has two electron groups: one C−H single bond and the C≡C triple bond (one group), no lone pairs → AX₂, linear. The whole molecule is a straight line.",["Count groups on one carbon.","The triple bond is one group.","Two groups → 180°."]],
["m9-a-081",9,18,3,"Concept","mcq","Why do lone pairs occupy EQUATORIAL positions in a trigonal bipyramidal arrangement (as in SF₄)?",["Equatorial positions are farther from the nucleus","Axial positions are reserved for the most electronegative atoms","Lone pairs are smaller than bonding pairs, so they fit in the equator","An equatorial position has only two neighbours at 90°, while an axial position has three, so a lone pair there feels less repulsion","The equatorial angle of 120° is smaller than the axial angle"],3,"Repulsions at 90° are the strongest. An axial site is 90° from three equatorial sites; an equatorial site is 90° from only the two axial sites (and 120° from the other two equatorial ones). Lone pairs, which repel most, therefore go equatorial: SF₄ seesaw, ClF₃ T-shaped, XeF₂ linear. Lone pairs are larger (more spread out), not smaller; 120° is larger than 90°.",["Which interactions are worse: at 90° or at 120°?","Count the 90° neighbours of an axial vs an equatorial site.","Equatorial: only two 90° neighbours."]],
["m9-a-082",9,18,3,"Concept","mcq","In XeF₄, where are the two lone pairs on Xe?",["Adjacent (90° apart), giving a seesaw shape","In two equatorial positions of a trigonal bipyramid","Adjacent (90° apart), giving a square pyramidal shape","Shared between two of the F atoms","Opposite each other (180° apart), giving a square planar shape"],4,"With six groups (octahedral), two lone pairs 90° apart would repel each other strongly; placing them 180° apart minimizes repulsion, leaving the four F atoms in a square plane. XeF₄ has six groups, not five, so a trigonal bipyramid does not apply.",["How many electron groups surround Xe?","Lone pairs keep as far apart as possible.","180° apart in an octahedron."]],
["m9-a-083",9,18,2,"Concept","mcq","Which AXₘEₙ designation gives a seesaw molecular shape?",["AX₃E","AX₃E₂","AX₄E","AX₂E₂","AX₄E₂"],2,"Seesaw = 5 electron groups with 4 bonds and 1 lone pair (AX₄E, e.g. SF₄). AX₃E is trigonal pyramidal, AX₃E₂ T-shaped, AX₂E₂ bent, AX₄E₂ square planar.",["Seesaw comes from the trigonal bipyramid family (5 groups).","One position is a lone pair.","4 atoms + 1 lone pair."]],
["m9-a-084",9,18,2,"Concept","mcq","What molecular shape does an AX₃E₂ molecule have?",["Trigonal planar","T-shaped","Trigonal pyramidal","Seesaw","Bent"],1,"AX₃E₂ has 5 groups (trigonal bipyramidal arrangement). The two lone pairs take equatorial positions, and the three atoms form a T (example: ClF₃). Trigonal pyramidal is AX₃E; trigonal planar is AX₃.",["Total groups = 3 + 2.","Five groups → trigonal bipyramid; lone pairs equatorial.","Example: ClF₃."]],
["m9-a-085",9,18,2,"Concept","mcq","Which species has a molecular shape that is DIFFERENT from its electron-group arrangement?",["NH₃","CH₄","BF₃","PCl₅","SF₆"],0,"The two differ only when the central atom has lone pairs. NH₃ (one lone pair) has a tetrahedral arrangement but a trigonal pyramidal shape. CH₄, BF₃, PCl₅ and SF₆ have no lone pairs on the central atom, so shape = arrangement.",["When do shape and arrangement differ?","Look for lone pairs on the central atom.","Only one choice has a lone pair on the centre."]],
["m9-a-086",9,18,3,"Concept","mcq","Which pair of species has the SAME molecular shape?",["NO₃⁻ and BF₃","NH₃ and BF₃","H₂O and CO₂","SO₂ and CO₂","ClO₃⁻ and SO₃"],0,"NO₃⁻ and BF₃ are both AX₃ → trigonal planar. NH₃ (pyramidal) vs BF₃ (planar); H₂O (bent) vs CO₂ (linear); SO₂ (bent) vs CO₂ (linear); ClO₃⁻ (pyramidal, lone pair on Cl) vs SO₃ (planar).",["Find the AXₘEₙ type of each species.","A lone pair on the centre changes the shape.","Two AX₃ species."]],
["m9-a-087",9,18,3,"Exam","mcq","Which species is NOT linear?",["CO₂","SO₂","XeF₂","I₃⁻","HCN"],1,"SO₂ is AX₂E (one lone pair on S) → bent. CO₂ and HCN are AX₂ (linear); XeF₂ and I₃⁻ are AX₂E₃, which is also linear because the three lone pairs are equatorial.",["Find the number of lone pairs on each central atom.","AX₂ and AX₂E₃ are both linear.","Which one is AX₂E?"]],
["m9-a-088",9,18,3,"Exam","mcq","Which species does NOT have a tetrahedral electron-group arrangement?",["NH₃","H₂O","ClO₃⁻","SO₃","NH₄⁺"],3,"Four electron groups give a tetrahedral arrangement: NH₃ (3 bonds + 1 lone pair), H₂O (2 + 2), ClO₃⁻ (3 + 1) and NH₄⁺ (4 + 0) all have four. SO₃ has three groups (three bonds, no lone pair on S) → trigonal planar.",["Count electron groups (bonds + lone pairs) on each centre.","Tetrahedral = 4 groups.","Which centre has only three groups?"]],
["m9-a-089",9,18,4,"Exam","mcq","Which TWO species are both square planar?",["XeF₄ and SF₄","CCl₄ and ICl₄⁻","BrF₅ and XeF₄","SF₄ and CH₄","XeF₄ and ICl₄⁻"],4,"Square planar = AX₄E₂. XeF₄ (36 e⁻) and ICl₄⁻ (36 e⁻) both have four bonds and two lone pairs on the centre. SF₄ is AX₄E (seesaw), CCl₄ and CH₄ are AX₄ (tetrahedral), BrF₅ is AX₅E (square pyramidal).",["Square planar needs six groups with two lone pairs.","Check each species for 4 bonds + 2 lone pairs.","Both have 36 valence electrons."]],
["m9-a-090",9,18,2,"Calculation","num","How many electron groups surround S in SF₄? (Enter a whole number.)",["5"],5,"SF₄ has 34 valence electrons: four S−F bonds and one lone pair on S. Electron groups = 4 + 1 = 5 (trigonal bipyramidal arrangement). Forgetting the lone pair gives 4.",["Count bonds on S.","Then check for a lone pair: 34 − 8 − 24.","4 bonds + 1 lone pair."],{"abs":0}],
["m9-a-091",9,18,3,"Calculation","num","How many lone pairs are on the central I atom in IF₅? (Enter a whole number.)",["1"],1,"IF₅: 7 + 35 = 42 valence electrons. Five I−F bonds use 10, three lone pairs on each F use 30; 42 − 40 = 2 electrons = 1 lone pair on I. So IF₅ is AX₅E, square pyramidal.",["Total valence electrons: 7 + 5(7).","Subtract bonding electrons and the F lone pairs.","42 − 10 − 30."],{"abs":0}],
["m9-a-092",9,18,3,"Exam","mcq","Which describes the phosphate ion, PO₄³⁻?",["32 valence electrons; square planar; 90°","29 valence electrons; tetrahedral; 109.5°","32 valence electrons; tetrahedral; O−P−O angles of 109.5°","32 valence electrons; trigonal pyramidal; 107°","26 valence electrons; trigonal planar; 120°"],2,"5 + 4(6) + 3 = 32 electrons. P has four bonds to O and no lone pair: AX₄ → tetrahedral, 109.5°. 29 subtracts the 3− charge instead of adding it; square planar would need two lone pairs; trigonal pyramidal would need a lone pair on P.",["Add 3 electrons for the 3− charge.","Count groups on P.","Four bonds, no lone pairs."]],
["m9-a-093",9,18,4,"Trap","mcq","Which describes the sulfite ion, SO₃²⁻?",["26 valence electrons; trigonal pyramidal","24 valence electrons; trigonal planar","26 valence electrons; trigonal planar","24 valence electrons; trigonal pyramidal","28 valence electrons; T-shaped"],0,"6 + 18 + 2 = 26 electrons. Three S−O bonds and the O lone pairs use 24, leaving one lone pair on S: AX₃E → trigonal pyramidal. 24 electrons and trigonal planar describe neutral SO₃ (no lone pair on S); the 2− charge adds the two electrons that form the lone pair.",["Add 2 electrons for the 2− charge.","Do the two extra electrons end up on S?","Three bonds + one lone pair."]],
["m9-a-094",9,19,1,"Concept","mcq","What is the approximate H−N−H bond angle in NH₃?",["109.5°","120°","104.5°","107°","90°"],3,"NH₃ has a tetrahedral arrangement (ideal 109.5°), but its lone pair repels the bonding pairs more strongly and squeezes the H−N−H angle to about 107°. 104.5° is water (two lone pairs); 120° is trigonal planar.",["Start from the tetrahedral angle.","One lone pair compresses the angle slightly.","A little less than 109.5°."]],
["m9-a-095",9,19,1,"Concept","mcq","What is the approximate H−O−H bond angle in H₂O?",["109.5°","107°","104.5°","120°","180°"],2,"Water: four groups (tetrahedral, ideal 109.5°) with two lone pairs, which compress the angle more than NH₃'s single lone pair: 104.5°. 180° would be a linear molecule (ignoring the lone pairs).",["Water has two lone pairs on O.","Two lone pairs squeeze more than one.","Less than NH₃'s 107°."]],
["m9-a-096",9,19,2,"Concept","mcq","Why is the H−O−H angle in water smaller than 109.5°?",["Bonding pairs repel more strongly than lone pairs","Water's electron-group arrangement is trigonal planar","Oxygen is a larger atom than carbon","The two hydrogen atoms attract each other","Lone pairs repel more strongly than bonding pairs, pushing the bonding pairs closer together"],4,"A lone pair is held by only one nucleus, so it spreads out and repels more than a bonding pair. Water's two lone pairs push the two O−H bonds together: 104.5°. The arrangement is tetrahedral (4 groups), and the H atoms (both δ+) repel, not attract.",["Compare lone-pair and bonding-pair repulsion.","Which takes up more room around the central atom?","Lone pairs repel more."]],
["m9-a-097",9,19,3,"Exam","mcq","Arrange in order of DECREASING H−X−H bond angle: H₂O, CH₄, NH₃.",["H₂O > NH₃ > CH₄","CH₄ > NH₃ > H₂O","NH₃ > CH₄ > H₂O","CH₄ > H₂O > NH₃","CH₄ = NH₃ = H₂O"],1,"All three have 4 electron groups (tetrahedral arrangement). More lone pairs → more compression: CH₄ (0 lone pairs) 109.5° > NH₃ (1) 107° > H₂O (2) 104.5°. They are not equal even though the arrangement is the same.",["All three have four electron groups.","Count lone pairs on each central atom.","0, 1, 2 lone pairs."]],
["m9-a-098",9,19,2,"Concept","mcq","The O−S−O bond angle in SO₂ is best described as:",["exactly 180°","slightly less than 120°","exactly 120°","slightly less than 109.5°","exactly 90°"],1,"SO₂ is AX₂E: three electron groups (trigonal planar arrangement, ideal 120°), and the lone pair on S compresses the angle a little (≈ 119°). 180° would be linear like CO₂; 109.5° belongs to four groups.",["How many electron groups are on S?","Three groups → ideal 120°.","A lone pair squeezes it slightly."]],
["m9-a-099",9,19,3,"Exam","mcq","What are the F−Cl−F bond angles between the axial and equatorial F atoms in ClF₃ (T-shaped)?",["120°","Exactly 180°","Slightly less than 90°","109.5°","Slightly more than 120°"],2,"ClF₃ is AX₃E₂ in a trigonal bipyramidal arrangement: the axial F atoms are ideally 90° from the equatorial F, and the two equatorial lone pairs push the axial F atoms toward the equatorial one, giving about 87.5°. (The angle between the two axial F atoms is slightly less than 180°.)",["T-shaped comes from a trigonal bipyramid.","Axial-to-equatorial is ideally 90°.","Lone pairs compress it."]],
["m9-a-100",9,19,3,"Concept","mcq","In formaldehyde, H₂C=O, the H−C−H angle is:",["slightly less than 120°, because the C=O double bond repels the C−H bonds more than they repel each other","exactly 109.5°","slightly more than 120°, because the H atoms repel each other strongly","exactly 180°","slightly less than 109.5°, because of a lone pair on C"],0,"C has three groups (trigonal planar, ideal 120°). The C=O double bond has more electron density than a single bond, so it pushes the two C−H bonds closer: H−C−H ≈ 116°. C has no lone pair; there are only three groups, so 109.5° does not apply.",["How many groups on C, and what is the ideal angle?","Double bonds repel a little more than single bonds.","The H−C−H angle shrinks below 120°."]],
["m9-a-101",9,19,3,"Exam","mcq","In methanol, H₃C−O−H, the C−O−H bond angle is closest to:",["120°","180°","exactly 109.5°","90°","slightly less than 109.5° (about 105-108°)"],4,"The O atom has two bonds (to C and H) and two lone pairs: four groups, AX₂E₂, bent like water. The ideal tetrahedral angle 109.5° is compressed by the two lone pairs to slightly less than 109.5° (measured ≈ 108°). 180° ignores the lone pairs; 120° would need only three groups.",["Look at the oxygen atom only.","Two bonds + two lone pairs on O.","Water-like: a bit less than 109.5°."]],
["m9-a-102",9,19,4,"Exam","mcq","In acetic acid, CH₃COOH (H₃C−C(=O)−O−H), what are the approximate bond angles around (1) the CH₃ carbon, (2) the carbon bonded to both O atoms, and (3) the O bonded to H?",["109.5°; 109.5°; 180°","120°; 120°; 120°","109.5°; 180°; 109.5°","109.5°; 120°; slightly less than 109.5°","120°; 109.5°; slightly less than 120°"],3,"(1) The CH₃ carbon has four single bonds → AX₄, 109.5°. (2) The carboxyl carbon has three groups (C−C, C=O, C−O) and no lone pair → AX₃, 120°. (3) The O−H oxygen has two bonds and two lone pairs → AX₂E₂, bent, slightly less than 109.5°. Analyze each centre separately; a molecule can have several different geometries.",["Treat each central atom on its own.","Count groups: 4 on CH₃ carbon, 3 on the C=O carbon, 4 on the OH oxygen.","Tetrahedral, trigonal planar, bent."]],
["m9-a-103",9,19,4,"Exam","mcq","In glycine, H₂N−CH₂−COOH, what is the geometry around the N atom, and the approximate H−N−H angle?",["Trigonal planar; 120°","Trigonal pyramidal; about 107°","Tetrahedral; 109.5°","Linear; 180°","Bent; about 104.5°"],1,"The N in the −NH₂ group is bonded to two H atoms and the CH₂ carbon and has one lone pair: AX₃E, trigonal pyramidal like NH₃, angles ≈ 107°. Counting only the two H atoms (and forgetting the bond to C) leads to 'bent'; ignoring the lone pair gives planar or tetrahedral.",["N has three bonds in glycine - two to H and one to C.","Plus one lone pair.","Like NH₃."]],
["m9-a-104",9,19,3,"Exam","mcq","In glycine, H₂N−CH₂−COOH, what are the ideal bond angles around the CH₂ carbon and around the carboxyl (COOH) carbon?",["109.5° and 120°","120° and 109.5°","109.5° and 109.5°","120° and 180°","180° and 120°"],0,"The CH₂ carbon has four single bonds (N, two H, C) → tetrahedral, 109.5°. The carboxyl carbon has three groups (C−C, C=O, C−OH) and no lone pair → trigonal planar, 120°.",["Count electron groups on each carbon.","Four single bonds vs one double + two single bonds.","4 groups and 3 groups."]],
["m9-a-105",9,19,2,"Concept","mcq","In a trigonal bipyramidal molecule such as PCl₅, what is the angle between an axial and an equatorial bond?",["120°","109.5°","180°","90°","72°"],3,"The axial bonds lie along the vertical axis and the three equatorial bonds lie in the horizontal plane: axial-equatorial = 90°, equatorial-equatorial = 120°, axial-axial = 180°.",["Axial bonds are on the vertical axis.","Equatorial bonds are in a horizontal plane.","Perpendicular."]],
["m9-a-106",9,19,2,"Concept","mcq","What is the F−Xe−F bond angle in XeF₂?",["120°","90°","109.5°","About 105°","180°"],4,"XeF₂ is AX₂E₃: the three lone pairs are equatorial and the two F atoms are axial, directly opposite each other → 180°. The lone pairs are spread symmetrically around the equator, so they do not bend the molecule.",["Where do three lone pairs go in a trigonal bipyramid?","The F atoms take the two axial positions.","Axial-to-axial."]],
["m9-a-107",9,19,3,"Exam","mcq","Which molecule has the SMALLEST ideal angle between adjacent bonds around its central atom?",["CO₂","BF₃","SF₆","CH₄","BeCl₂"],2,"Ideal angles: CO₂ and BeCl₂ 180° (linear), BF₃ 120° (trigonal planar), CH₄ 109.5° (tetrahedral), SF₆ 90° (octahedral). More electron groups → smaller angles.",["Find the electron-group arrangement of each.","More groups crowd closer together.","Six groups → 90°."]],
["m9-a-108",9,19,1,"Concept","mcq","A molecule is polar if:",["it contains at least one polar bond","its central atom has an expanded octet","all of its bonds are nonpolar","it contains a double bond","it has polar bonds arranged so that their bond dipoles do not cancel"],4,"Two conditions: (1) polar bonds, and (2) a shape in which the bond dipoles (and lone pairs) do not cancel. CO₂ has polar bonds but is nonpolar because its dipoles cancel, so 'at least one polar bond' is not enough.",["Polar bonds alone are not enough.","Think about CO₂.","Bond polarity AND shape."]],
["m9-a-109",9,19,2,"Concept","mcq","Why is CO₂ nonpolar even though C=O bonds are polar?",["C=O bonds are actually nonpolar","C and O have the same electronegativity","Its two C=O bond dipoles are equal and point in opposite directions (linear), so they cancel","A lone pair on C cancels the bond dipoles","Double bonds are never polar"],2,"O (3.5) is more electronegative than C (2.5), so each C=O bond is polar. But CO₂ is linear (AX₂): the two equal dipoles point in opposite directions and cancel, giving zero overall dipole. C has no lone pair in CO₂.",["Is each C=O bond polar?","What is the shape of CO₂?","Equal and opposite dipoles."]],
["m9-a-110",9,19,3,"Exam","mcq","SO₂ is polar but CO₂ is not. Why?",["S−O bonds are polar but C=O bonds are not","SO₂ has a larger molar mass","S is more electronegative than O","SO₂ is bent (a lone pair on S), so its bond dipoles do not cancel; CO₂ is linear, so its dipoles cancel","CO₂ has resonance structures and SO₂ does not"],3,"Both have polar bonds. The difference is shape: S in SO₂ has a lone pair (AX₂E, bent ≈ 119°), so the two S−O dipoles add to a net dipole; CO₂ (AX₂) is linear and they cancel. Mass does not decide polarity; O is more electronegative than S; SO₂ does have resonance.",["Compare the shapes, not the bonds.","Does S in SO₂ have a lone pair?","Bent vs linear."]],
["m9-a-111",9,19,3,"Exam","mcq","BF₃ is nonpolar, but NF₃ is polar. Why?",["NF₃ has a lone pair on N, making it trigonal pyramidal so its dipoles do not cancel; BF₃ is trigonal planar and its three B−F dipoles cancel","B−F bonds are nonpolar but N−F bonds are polar","NF₃ has an expanded octet","BF₃ has a lone pair on B that cancels the dipoles","F is more electronegative in NF₃ than in BF₃"],0,"BF₃: AX₃, trigonal planar, three equal B−F dipoles at 120° cancel. NF₃: 26 electrons, AX₃E, trigonal pyramidal, the N−F dipoles do not cancel → polar. B−F bonds are very polar (ΔEN 2.0); N has an octet; B has no lone pair; F's electronegativity does not change.",["Count lone pairs on B and on N.","Planar vs pyramidal.","Symmetric shapes cancel; pyramids do not."]],
["m9-a-112",9,19,2,"Exam","mcq","Which molecule is polar?",["CCl₄","CH₃Cl","CH₄","CO₂","BF₃"],1,"CH₃Cl is tetrahedral but has one C−Cl bond and three C−H bonds; the dipoles are not identical, so they do not cancel → polar. CCl₄ and CH₄ (four identical bonds), CO₂ (linear) and BF₃ (trigonal planar) all cancel.",["Look for a molecule whose outer atoms are not all the same.","Symmetric shape + identical outer atoms → nonpolar.","One different outer atom breaks the symmetry."]],
["m9-a-113",9,19,3,"Trap","mcq","CH₃Cl and CCl₄ both have a tetrahedral shape. Which statement is correct?",["CH₃Cl is polar because its one C−Cl dipole is not balanced by the C−H bonds; CCl₄ is nonpolar because four identical dipoles cancel","Both are nonpolar, because tetrahedral molecules are always nonpolar","Both are polar, because both contain polar C−Cl bonds","CCl₄ is polar and CH₃Cl is nonpolar","CH₃Cl is nonpolar because C−H bonds are nonpolar"],0,"A symmetric shape cancels dipoles only when the outer atoms are identical. In CCl₄ four equal C−Cl dipoles cancel → nonpolar. In CH₃Cl the C−Cl dipole is much larger than the C−H dipoles, so there is a net dipole → polar. Shape alone or bond polarity alone does not decide it.",["Check both steps: polar bonds, then whether they cancel.","Are the four outer atoms the same in each?","Different outer atoms → net dipole."]],
["m9-a-114",9,19,4,"Exam","mcq","For the two isomers of 1,2-dichloroethene, ClHC=CHCl, which statement is correct?",["Both isomers are nonpolar","The cis isomer (both Cl on the same side) is polar; the trans isomer (Cl on opposite sides) is nonpolar","Both isomers are polar","The trans isomer is polar; the cis isomer is nonpolar","Neither isomer has polar bonds"],1,"Each C is trigonal planar and the molecule is flat. In trans, the two C−Cl dipoles point in exactly opposite directions and cancel (as do the C−H dipoles) → nonpolar. In cis, both C−Cl dipoles point toward the same side, giving a net dipole → polar. Same formula, same bonds, different polarity.",["Sketch both isomers in a plane.","Do the two C−Cl dipoles point opposite ways or the same way?","Opposite cancels (trans)."]],
["m9-a-115",9,19,4,"Trap","mcq","XeF₄ has polar Xe−F bonds and two lone pairs on Xe. Is XeF₄ polar?",["Yes, because any molecule with lone pairs on the central atom is polar","Yes, because Xe−F bonds are polar","No, because Xe−F bonds are nonpolar","No; in its square planar shape the Xe−F dipoles (and the two lone pairs) are arranged opposite each other, so they cancel","Yes, because XeF₄ is seesaw-shaped"],3,"XeF₄ is AX₄E₂, square planar: each Xe−F dipole has an equal one directly opposite, and the two lone pairs sit 180° apart, so everything cancels → nonpolar. Lone pairs do not automatically make a molecule polar (XeF₂ and XeF₄ are nonpolar); the bonds are polar but cancel; seesaw is SF₄.",["Find the shape first (AX₄E₂).","Are the lone pairs and the dipoles symmetric?","Opposite pairs cancel."]],
["m9-a-116",9,19,3,"Exam","mcq","Which molecule is NONPOLAR?",["PCl₃","SF₄","ClF₃","BrF₅","PCl₅"],4,"PCl₅ is AX₅, trigonal bipyramidal with five identical P−Cl bonds; the axial dipoles cancel each other and the three equatorial dipoles cancel at 120° → nonpolar. PCl₃ (pyramidal), SF₄ (seesaw), ClF₃ (T-shaped) and BrF₅ (square pyramidal) each have a lone pair arrangement that leaves a net dipole.",["Which central atom has no lone pairs?","Symmetric shape + identical outer atoms → nonpolar.","Its VSEPR class is AX₅."]],
["m9-a-117",9,19,3,"Exam","mcq","Which set contains ONLY polar molecules?",["CO₂, H₂O, NH₃","BF₃, NF₃, SO₂","H₂O, NH₃, SO₂","CH₄, CH₃Cl, CHCl₃","XeF₂, SF₄, ClF₃"],2,"H₂O (bent), NH₃ (pyramidal) and SO₂ (bent) are all polar. Each wrong set contains one nonpolar molecule: CO₂ (linear), BF₃ (trigonal planar), CH₄ (tetrahedral, identical bonds) and XeF₂ (linear).",["Check every molecule in each set.","One nonpolar member spoils a set.","Look for linear, trigonal planar or fully symmetric members."]],
["m9-a-118",9,19,4,"Exam","mcq","Which set contains ONLY nonpolar molecules?",["CO₂, BF₃, XeF₄","CO₂, SO₂, XeF₄","BF₃, NF₃, CH₄","CCl₄, CH₂Cl₂, SF₆","SF₄, SF₆, XeF₂"],0,"CO₂ (linear), BF₃ (trigonal planar) and XeF₄ (square planar) all have cancelling dipoles. Each wrong set has one polar member: SO₂ (bent), NF₃ (pyramidal), CH₂Cl₂ (tetrahedral but with two different kinds of outer atom) and SF₄ (seesaw).",["Check the shape of every molecule.","Bent, pyramidal, seesaw and mixed-substituent molecules are polar.","Find the set with no such member."]],
["m9-a-119",9,19,3,"Concept","mcq","HCN is linear. Is it polar?",["No, linear molecules are always nonpolar","Yes; the H−C and C≡N dipoles both point toward the N end, so they add rather than cancel","No, because the C≡N bond is nonpolar","Yes, because C has a lone pair","No, because the H and N ends cancel each other"],1,"A linear molecule is nonpolar only if its two ends are identical (like CO₂). In H−C≡N, C (2.5) is more electronegative than H (2.1), and N (3.0) more than C, so both dipoles point from H toward N; they add → polar. C has no lone pair in HCN.",["Are the two ends of the molecule the same?","Find the δ− end of each bond.","Both dipoles point toward N."]],
["m9-a-120",9,19,3,"Exam","mcq","Is dichloromethane, CH₂Cl₂, polar?",["No, because it is tetrahedral","No, because the two C−Cl dipoles cancel each other in a flat square","Yes; the tetrahedral shape cannot cancel two C−Cl dipoles against two much smaller C−H dipoles","Yes, because C has a lone pair","No, because C−Cl bonds are nonpolar"],2,"CH₂Cl₂ is tetrahedral (not a flat square, which is only how it looks on paper). The two C−Cl dipoles point to two corners and the two small C−H dipoles to the other two, so there is a net dipole → polar. C has no lone pair; C−Cl is polar (ΔEN 0.5).",["Is the molecule flat or tetrahedral?","Are all four outer atoms identical?","Different outer atoms on a tetrahedron → polar."]],
["m9-a-121",9,19,4,"Exam","mcq","Which choice correctly gives the total valence electrons, molecular shape and polarity of SF₄?",["34; tetrahedral; nonpolar","32; tetrahedral; nonpolar","34; seesaw; nonpolar","34; square planar; nonpolar","34; seesaw; polar"],4,"6 + 4(7) = 34 electrons → four S−F bonds and one lone pair on S (AX₄E) → seesaw. The lone pair makes the shape unsymmetrical, so the S−F dipoles do not cancel → polar. 'Tetrahedral, nonpolar' forgets the lone pair (32 is the count with no lone pair on S).",["Count valence electrons: 6 + 28.","Is there a lone pair on S?","AX₄E → seesaw → does it cancel?"]],
["m9-a-122",9,19,4,"Exam","mcq","Which choice correctly gives the molecular shape and polarity of BrF₅?",["Trigonal bipyramidal; nonpolar","Square pyramidal; nonpolar","Octahedral; nonpolar","Square pyramidal; polar","Square planar; polar"],3,"BrF₅: 42 electrons → five Br−F bonds and one lone pair (AX₅E) → square pyramidal. The four basal Br−F dipoles cancel each other, but the apical Br−F dipole is not balanced (the lone pair is opposite it) → polar. Trigonal bipyramidal and octahedral ignore the lone pair.",["Count valence electrons and lone pairs on Br.","6 groups, one a lone pair → square pyramidal.","The apical bond has nothing opposite to cancel it."]],
["m9-a-123",9,19,3,"Concept","mcq","Is 1,1-dichloroethene, Cl₂C=CH₂, polar?",["Yes; both C−Cl dipoles are on the same end of the molecule, so they add","No, because it has the same formula as trans-1,2-dichloroethene","No, because the C=C double bond cancels the C−Cl dipoles","No, because planar molecules are always nonpolar","Yes, because each carbon has a lone pair"],0,"Both Cl atoms are on one carbon, so both C−Cl dipoles point toward the same end of the flat molecule (with the C−H bonds at the other end) → net dipole → polar. Same formula as trans-1,2-dichloroethene does not mean same polarity; planarity alone does not cancel dipoles; neither carbon has a lone pair.",["Where are the two Cl atoms?","Do the two C−Cl dipoles point toward the same end or opposite ends?","Same end → they add."]],
["m9-a-124",9,19,2,"Trap","mcq","Which statement about molecular polarity is FALSE?",["A diatomic molecule of two identical atoms, such as Cl₂, is nonpolar","Every molecule that contains polar bonds is polar","A bent triatomic molecule with polar bonds is polar","Lone pairs on the central atom often make a molecule polar","A molecule can have polar bonds yet be nonpolar"],1,"CO₂, BF₃, CCl₄ and SF₆ all contain polar bonds but are nonpolar because their bond dipoles cancel, so 'every molecule with polar bonds is polar' is false. The other statements are true (two identical atoms share electrons equally, so Cl₂ has no dipole; bent molecules like H₂O and SO₂ are polar; lone pairs usually unbalance the shape, though not in XeF₂ or XeF₄).",["Think of CO₂.","Polar bonds can cancel.","Find the overgeneralization."]]
]);
